consiSt("in",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Indiana Supreme Court","d":"Has mandatory and exclusive jurisdiction over death-penalty appeals, appeals of final judgments declaring a state or federal statute unconstitutional, parental-consent-to-abortion waiver appeals, and mandate-of-funds appeals. Everything else reaches it by discretionary transfer from the Court of Appeals (App.R. 57) or review of the Tax Court (App.R. 63). It has exclusive control of bar admission (including temporary admission), lawyer discipline, judicial discipline, and supervision of lower courts, including writs of mandate and prohibition. Its Clerk is also the Clerk of the Court of Appeals and Tax Court."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Indiana Court of Appeals","d":"Hears all appeals from final judgments of circuit, superior, probate, and county courts not reserved to the Supreme Court, interlocutory appeals under App.R. 14, and review of final agency decisions. It decides most cases by memorandum decision; only opinions meeting App.R. 65(A) criteria are published."},{"l":"SPECIAL","n":"Indiana Tax Court","d":"Specialized court whose decisions are reviewed by the Supreme Court on a petition for review under App.R. 63, not by the Court of Appeals."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit, Superior, and Probate Courts","d":"The general trial courts, organized by county. Any case may be filed in any county (T.R. 75(A)), but a party can force transfer to a county of ‘preferred venue.’ Special judges for these courts are chosen under T.R. 79 and local rules approved by the Supreme Court."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"City and town courts; Marion County small claims courts","d":"Limited-jurisdiction courts with their own special-judge selection rule (T.R. 79.1). Small claims procedure is governed by separate Small Claims Rules, which were not collected."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute: Ind. Code ch. 34-7-7 (1998)","d":"Indiana’s anti-SLAPP law makes it a defense to a civil action that the act or omission complained of was (1) in furtherance of the person’s right of petition or free speech under the U.S. or Indiana Constitution in connection with a public issue or issue of public interest, and (2) taken in good faith and with a reasonable basis in law and fact. ‘Claim’ includes a lawsuit, cause of action, petition, complaint, cross-claim, counterclaim, or any other pleading seeking legal or equitable relief, and ‘person’ includes any legal entity. The chapter does not apply to enforcement actions brought for the State by the attorney general, a prosecutor, or another public prosecutor. The chapter has not been amended since it was added by P.L.114-1998 (it is not a UPEPA statute).","c":"Ind. Code §§ 34-7-7-1 to -5, -10","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: motion to dismiss treated as summary judgment; 180-day outer limit","d":"The defendant raises the defense by a motion to dismiss under the chapter, which must state with specificity the public issue or issue of public interest that prompted the act. The court must treat it as a summary-judgment motion, set a reasonable expedited period of no more than 180 days to rule, and set limits for discovery needed to respond. Filing the motion stays all discovery except discovery relevant to the motion. The court decides on the pleadings and affidavits filed and discovered in the expedited proceeding, and must grant the motion if the movant proves by a preponderance of the evidence that the act on which the claim is based is a lawful act in furtherance of petition or speech rights. The statute sets no deadline for filing the motion; check case law on timing before relying on a late filing.","c":"Ind. Code §§ 34-7-7-6, -9(a)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory fees for a prevailing defendant; fees against a frivolous movant","d":"A defendant who prevails on the motion is entitled to reasonable attorney’s fees and costs. If the court finds the motion frivolous or solely intended to cause unnecessary delay, the plaintiff is entitled to reasonable fees and costs to answer it.","c":"Ind. Code §§ 34-7-7-7, -8","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: only an express right if the court fails to rule in 30 days","d":"The court must act within 30 days after the submission of the evidence discovered in the expedited period. If it does not, the movant may appeal based on the failure to rule. The chapter gives no express interlocutory appeal from an order denying the motion; absent another route, a denial would need trial-court certification and Court of Appeals acceptance under App.R. 14(B). Confirm current case law on appealability before advising.","c":"Ind. Code § 34-7-7-9(e)–(f); Ind. App. R. 14(B)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Temporary admission (pro hac vice): Admis. Disc. R. 3, § 2","d":"Any Indiana court may, in its discretion and for good cause, let a lawyer admitted in another U.S. state, territory, or D.C. appear in a particular case. Conditions: (1) an Indiana lawyer has appeared and agreed to act as co-counsel; (2) the applicant is not an Indiana resident and is not regularly employed or regularly engaged in business or professional activities in Indiana; (3) the applicant pays a $300 case-specific registration fee to the Executive Director of the Indiana Office of Admissions and Continuing Education, with a copy of the petition, and receives a temporary admission attorney number (the fee is not refunded if the petition is denied); and (4) the applicant files a certificate of good standing issued within the previous 30 days by the highest court of every admitting state, plus a verified petition co-signed by Indiana co-counsel.","c":"Ind. Admis. Disc. R. 3, § 2(a)(1)–(4)","v":true},{"t":"What the verified petition must say; repeat appearances","d":"The petition lists contact and firm information, every jurisdiction of admission with dates and numbers, good standing, any past discipline (with reasons to admit anyway), any pending disciplinary proceeding (with a continuing duty to report), every Indiana case in the last 5 years in which the lawyer appeared by temporary admission, an agreement to be bound by the Indiana Rules of Professional Conduct and to submit to Indiana disciplinary jurisdiction, and the fee receipt and temporary attorney number. Absent good cause, repeated appearances are cause for denial; good cause includes special expertise in a complex field, a long client relationship, lack of local expertise, questions of the applicant’s home-state law, or a similar reason. The rule sets no numeric cap.","c":"Ind. Admis. Disc. R. 3, § 2(a)(4)(i)–(ix)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel duties; each lawyer pays for each case","d":"Indiana co-counsel must sign all briefs, papers, and pleadings and is jointly responsible for them; the signature certifies good ground and no improper purpose. Local counsel need not attend proceedings unless the trial court orders it. A foreign lawyer who fails to register or pay is subject to Indiana discipline, and so is local counsel if the admitted lawyer fails to pay or comply. The fee is per case, and each lawyer from the same firm must comply individually. A temporary admission granted in the Court of Appeals or Tax Court carries into the Supreme Court on transfer or review.","c":"Ind. Admis. Disc. R. 3, § 2(b)–(c) and notes; Ind. App. R. 16(F)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary and limited-scope appearances in trial courts","d":"A lawyer representing a party on a temporary or limited-scope basis files a notice of temporary or limited representation giving the appearance information and describing the end date or scope; the court need not act on it. The lawyer files a notice of completion when done. Every appearance form must include the attorney’s Indiana attorney number, so get the temporary admission number first.","c":"Ind. T.R. 3.1(A)(2), (I)","v":true},{"t":"Other paths: admission without examination, business counsel, temporary license","d":"A lawyer actively practicing at least 3 of the last 5 years (1,000 hours a year for most categories) may be admitted without examination. In-house lawyers who establish an Indiana presence for an employer that is not a law practice may obtain a business counsel license, which ends if the employment moves out of Indiana or ends (unless new qualifying employment begins within 3 months). Rule 6.1 temporary licenses (up to 2 years, pending a bar application) are limited to law-school clinic faculty, legal services and public defender lawyers, pro bono volunteers through those organizations, Department of Child Services litigators, and deputy prosecutors.","c":"Ind. Admis. Disc. R. 6, §§ 1–2; Ind. Admis. Disc. R. 6.1","v":true},{"t":"Multijurisdictional practice without admission","d":"Indiana’s Rule 5.5 follows the ABA model: temporary services in association with an actively participating Indiana lawyer, services related to a proceeding in which the lawyer is or expects to be authorized to appear, ADR-related services, and services arising out of home-state practice are allowed, as are services to the lawyer’s employer if there is no systematic presence and the forum does not require temporary admission.","c":"Ind. Prof. Cond. R. 5.5(c)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory e-filing for lawyers (IEFS)","d":"Lawyers must e-file in trial courts through the Indiana E-Filing System (IEFS) after signing a user agreement with an approved e-filing service provider; an exemption requires a petition in each pending case, granted only for good cause. Unrepresented litigants are encouraged but not required to e-file. A filing counts for a day only if completed before midnight in the time zone of the court where the case is filed. Service on registered users is by E-Service through IEFS, complete on transmission. Lawyers must also e-file in the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals.","c":"Ind. T.R. 86(B)(3), 87(A), (B), (H); Ind. App. R. 68(A), (C)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Indiana Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"Indiana’s rules are based on the ABA Model Rules (adopted effective January 1, 1987; the collected compilation is updated effective October 1, 2024). The Supreme Court’s Disciplinary Commission enforces them under Admis. Disc. R. 23. The differences below come from the rule text.","c":"Ind. Prof. Cond. R. (preamble and Rules 1.0–8.5); Ind. Admis. Disc. R. 23","v":true},{"t":"Rule 2.2 ‘Intermediary’ is still in force","d":"The ABA deleted Model Rule 2.2 in 2002, but Indiana keeps it. A lawyer may act as intermediary between clients after consulting each client about the risks, advantages, and effect on privilege and getting each client’s consent, if the lawyer reasonably believes the matter can be resolved compatibly with each client’s best interests and handled impartially. The lawyer must withdraw if any client asks or a condition fails, and may not then represent any of them in that matter.","c":"Ind. Prof. Cond. R. 2.2","v":true},{"t":"Rule 8.4(g): bias in a professional capacity is misconduct","d":"It is misconduct to engage, in a professional capacity, in conduct manifesting bias or prejudice by words or conduct based on race, gender, religion, national origin, disability, sexual orientation, age, socioeconomic status, or similar factors. Legitimate advocacy is excepted, and a trial judge’s finding of discriminatory peremptory strikes does not by itself establish a violation. This is Indiana’s own wording, not the ABA’s 2016 Rule 8.4(g).","c":"Ind. Prof. Cond. R. 8.4(g)","v":true},{"t":"Targeted solicitation: ‘Advertising Material,’ filing with the Disciplinary Commission, $50 fee, 30-day accident ban","d":"Written, recorded, or electronic solicitations to a person known to need help in a particular matter must be labeled ‘Advertising Material’ (on the envelope and at the start, or at the beginning and end of a recording), and a copy must be filed with the Disciplinary Commission at or before dissemination with a $50 fee; each changed version must be filed too. The lawyer must keep a recipient list for at least 1 year. No solicitation about personal injury, wrongful death, or an accident or disaster is allowed until 30 days after the event.","c":"Ind. Prof. Cond. R. 7.3(b)(3), (c)","v":true},{"t":"Advertising records kept 6 years","d":"Every advertising communication must name at least one responsible lawyer or firm with an office address, and a copy or recording must be kept for 6 years after dissemination.","c":"Ind. Prof. Cond. R. 7.2(c)","v":true},{"t":"Contingent fees: post-judgment domestic collection allowed","d":"Contingent fee agreements must be in a writing signed by the client stating the percentages for settlement, trial, and appeal and how expenses are handled, with a closing statement at the end. Contingent fees in domestic relations matters are barred, but Indiana expressly allows one for a post-judgment collection action if the client is told in writing about alternative collection measures.","c":"Ind. Prof. Cond. R. 1.5(c)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: added disability exception","d":"Rule 1.6(b) tracks the ABA exceptions (death or bodily harm, client crime or fraud using the lawyer’s services, ethics advice, self-defense, other law or court order). Indiana adds Rule 1.6(c), permitting disclosure of client names and files as needed by the person managing the files of a disabled lawyer or by an appointed guardian or conservator of the client files.","c":"Ind. Prof. Cond. R. 1.6(b)–(c)","v":true}],"style":"State of Indiana","home":"https://www.in.gov/courts/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"The notice of appeal is filed with the appellate Clerk, not the trial court","d":"The notice of appeal is filed with the Clerk of the Indiana Supreme Court, Court of Appeals and Tax Court within 30 days after the final judgment is noted in the Chronological Case Summary, with the $250 fee. A late notice forfeits the appeal (except under Post-Conviction Rule 2).","c":"Ind. App. R. 2(D), 9(A)(1), (A)(5), 9(E)"},{"t":"Motion to correct error: deemed denial starts the clock","d":"After a timely motion to correct error, the notice of appeal is due 30 days after the ruling or 30 days after the motion is deemed denied under T.R. 53.3, whichever comes first. Do not wait for a written ruling after the deemed-denial date passes.","c":"Ind. App. R. 9(A)(1); Ind. T.R. 53.3(A)"},{"t":"Some issues must be raised in a motion to correct error","d":"A motion to correct error is generally optional, but it is required to raise newly discovered evidence (including jury misconduct) or a claim that a jury verdict is excessive or inadequate. It is due 30 days after entry of final judgment is noted in the CCS, and that time cannot be extended.","c":"Ind. T.R. 59(A), (C); Ind. T.R. 6(C)"},{"t":"Money-payment orders are appealable now, or the chance may be lost","d":"Interlocutory orders for the payment of money, to compel execution of a document or delivery of securities, for sale or delivery of real property, on preliminary injunctions and receivers, and transferring or refusing to transfer venue under T.R. 75 are appealable as of right within 30 days after the order is noted in the CCS. Other interlocutory orders need a motion for trial-court certification within 30 days and a motion in the Court of Appeals within 30 days after certification.","c":"Ind. App. R. 14(A), (B)(1)(a), (B)(2)(a)"},{"t":"Change-of-judge window is short and one-time","d":"The automatic change of judge must be sought within 10 days after the issues are first closed on the merits (typically the answer or reply). Miss it and you need a personally verified motion showing newly discovered grounds; each party gets only one change of judge and one change of county in the case.","c":"Ind. T.R. 76(B)–(D)"},{"t":"Automatic 30-day extension to answer, but only if noticed on time","d":"A party may get one automatic 30-day enlargement to respond to a complaint or other pleading by filing a notice stating the original and new due dates on or before the original due date. Responses to pleadings and motions are otherwise due 20 days after service, replies 14 days after the response, and 3 days are added only for service by U.S. mail.","c":"Ind. T.R. 6(B), (D), (G)"},{"t":"Midnight deadline in the court’s time zone","d":"Indiana has counties in two time zones, and an e-filing is timely only if completed before midnight in the time zone of the court where the case is filed (or pending, on appeal).","c":"Ind. T.R. 87(H); Ind. App. R. 68(I)"},{"t":"No extensions for rehearing or transfer petitions","d":"A petition for rehearing is due 30 days after the decision and a petition to transfer 45 days after the decision (or 30 days after a rehearing ruling). No extension is allowed, and the 3-day mail extension does not apply.","c":"Ind. App. R. 35(C), 54(B), 57(C)"},{"t":"Pre-2023 memorandum decisions are not citable","d":"Unpublished Court of Appeals memorandum decisions issued before January 1, 2023 may not be cited to any court except for res judicata, collateral estoppel, or law of the case. Those issued on or after January 1, 2023 may be cited for persuasive value. A party may move to publish within 15 days of the decision.","c":"Ind. App. R. 65(B), (D)"},{"t":"Summary judgment lives or dies on ‘designation’","d":"The opponent has 30 days to respond, each side must specifically designate the evidence it relies on, and the court considers only designated material. A summary-judgment ruling will not be reversed on a fact issue that was not specifically designated to the trial court.","c":"Ind. T.R. 56(C), (H)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice: $300 per case, good-standing certificates under 30 days old, residency bar","d":"Temporary admission requires Indiana co-counsel who signs everything, a $300 per-case fee paid before filing the petition, certificates of good standing from every admitting state issued within 30 days, and the applicant may not be an Indiana resident or regularly practicing or employed in Indiana.","c":"Ind. Admis. Disc. R. 3, § 2(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Discovery motions need a recited conference","d":"Before filing a discovery motion, a party must make a reasonable effort to resolve the dispute and state in the motion the date, time, and place of that effort and who took part, or the court may deny the motion. Discovery requests must also be served in an editable word-processing format.","c":"Ind. T.R. 26(F), (A)(1)"}],"judge":[{"t":"One automatic change of judge per party, then T.R. 79 special-judge selection","d":"In a civil case each party gets one change of judge, granted on an unverified motion that states no grounds. The motion is due within 10 days after the issues are first closed on the merits (30 days after filing where no responsive pleading closes the issues, and in probate, receivership, and remonstrance matters; 10 days after a new-trial order, remand, or transfer to a new case number), or later only on a motion personally verified by the party showing newly discovered grounds and why they could not have been found earlier. A party gets one change of county and one change of judge in the entire case, including later petitions to modify a decree. Within 7 days after the change is noted in the Chronological Case Summary, the parties may agree in writing on an eligible special judge, who has 7 days to accept; otherwise the special judge is chosen under a Supreme Court–approved local rule and must accept unless disqualified or excused. Separately, a judge must disqualify when impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including the listed relationships, economic interests, prior involvement, and public statements that commit the judge to a result.","c":"Ind. T.R. 76(B)–(D); Ind. T.R. 79(C), (D), (H); Ind. Code Jud. Conduct R. 2.11","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"No separate speech rule collected","d":"Beyond the anti-SLAPP statute (Ind. Code ch. 34-7-7), the collected texts contain no Indiana-specific speech protection to summarize. The Indiana Constitution’s free-speech clause and Indiana defamation statutes (Ind. Code art. 34-15) were not collected.","c":"None collected","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"The ‘lazy judge’ rule and deemed denials","d":"If a trial court fails for 30 days to set a motion for hearing, or fails to rule within 30 days after the hearing (or after filing if no hearing is required), a party may file a praecipe to withdraw submission and have a special judge appointed. The judge may extend once by 30 days by a CCS entry made before the deadline. Motions to correct error are deemed denied if not set within 45 days, or not ruled on within 30 days after the hearing or 45 days after filing when there is no hearing; repetitive motions and motions to reconsider are deemed denied if not ruled on within 5 days and do not extend any deadline. A deemed denial starts the 30-day appeal clock.","c":"Ind. T.R. 53.1, 53.3, 53.4; Ind. App. R. 9(A)(1)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Indiana Rules of Appellate Procedure (adopted effective January 1, 2001; updated effective July 1, 2026); Indiana Rules of Trial Procedure (updated effective August 1, 2026), Rules 53.3, 53.4, 59, 87; Ind. Code § 34-7-7-9. Official texts from rules.incourts.gov and iga.in.gov; summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Indiana: file with the appellate Clerk in 30 days, then most cases end in the Court of Appeals","d":"Almost every civil appeal goes to the Court of Appeals; the Supreme Court hears only a short list of mandatory appeals and otherwise acts by discretionary transfer. The appeal starts with a Notice of Appeal filed with the Clerk of the Indiana Supreme Court, Court of Appeals and Tax Court (not the trial court clerk) within 30 days after the final judgment is noted in the Chronological Case Summary (CCS). A late notice forfeits the appeal.\nTwo Indiana features drive the timing: the single post-judgment motion is the motion to correct error, which can be deemed denied by the passage of time, and several interlocutory orders (including orders for the payment of money) are appealable as of right within 30 days.","c":"Ind. App. R. 4, 5, 9(A); Ind. T.R. 53.3, 59"},"groups":[["Getting into the Court of Appeals",[{"t":"What is a final judgment","d":"A judgment is final if it disposes of all claims as to all parties; if the trial court in writing expressly finds no just reason for delay and expressly directs entry of judgment under T.R. 54(B) or 56(C) as to fewer than all claims or parties; if deemed final under T.R. 60(C); if it rules on a timely motion to correct error; or if otherwise deemed final by law.","c":"Ind. App. R. 2(H)"},{"t":"Notice of Appeal: where, when, and the fee","d":"File the Notice of Appeal with the appellate Clerk within 30 days after entry of the final judgment is noted in the CCS. Pay the $250 filing fee when the notice is filed (none for in forma pauperis or governmental appellants); the Clerk will not file other papers until it is paid. Unless the notice is timely, the right to appeal is forfeited except under Post-Conviction Rule 2.","c":"Ind. App. R. 9(A)(1), (A)(5), 9(E)"},{"t":"Contents of the notice","d":"The notice designates the order appealed and the basis for jurisdiction (final judgment, interlocutory appeal of right, accepted discretionary interlocutory appeal, or expedited appeal), directs assembly of the Clerk’s Record, and requests the Transcript; if you will argue a finding is unsupported by the evidence, request a Transcript of all the evidence. Attach the judgment (with findings and conclusions), the order on any motion to correct error (or the motion itself if deemed denied), and any sealing orders. Certify whether the case has priority issues, compliance with access-to-records rules, and that you will arrange transcript payment. In civil cases, state whether you are willing to take part in appellate ADR.","c":"Ind. App. R. 9(F)"},{"t":"Motion to correct error and the deemed-denial trap","d":"A motion to correct error is due 30 days after entry of the final judgment is noted in the CCS and cannot be extended. If one is timely filed, the Notice of Appeal is due 30 days after the ruling is noted in the CCS or 30 days after it is deemed denied under T.R. 53.3, whichever comes first. It is deemed denied if not set for hearing within 45 days, or not ruled on within 30 days after the hearing or 45 days after filing when no hearing is required, unless the judge extended the time once by up to 30 days by a CCS entry or the parties agreed on the record. Repetitive motions and motions to reconsider are deemed denied after 5 days and extend nothing.","c":"Ind. T.R. 59(C); Ind. T.R. 6(C); Ind. T.R. 53.3; Ind. T.R. 53.4; Ind. App. R. 9(A)(1)"},{"t":"When a motion to correct error is required","d":"It is optional for most issues, but it is a prerequisite to appeal for newly discovered material evidence (including jury misconduct) and for a claim that a jury verdict is excessive or inadequate.","c":"Ind. T.R. 59(A)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals and joint appeals","d":"An appellee may cross-appeal without filing a notice of appeal by raising cross-appeal issues in the appellee’s brief. But if no other party appeals, a party must file its own Notice of Appeal to preserve its right. Parties entitled to appeal from the same judgment may file a joint notice and proceed as one appellant.","c":"Ind. App. R. 9(C), (D)"},{"t":"Interlocutory appeals of right","d":"These orders are appealable as of right by a Notice of Appeal filed within 30 days after the order is noted in the CCS: orders for the payment of money; to compel execution of a document; to compel delivery or assignment of securities, evidence of debt, documents, or things in action; for the sale or delivery of possession of real property; granting, refusing, dissolving, or refusing to dissolve a preliminary injunction; appointing or refusing to appoint (or revoke) a receiver; certain habeas orders; transferring or refusing to transfer venue under T.R. 75; and agency orders a statute requires to be appealed interlocutorily.","c":"Ind. App. R. 14(A)"},{"t":"Discretionary interlocutory appeals: two motions","d":"Move the trial court to certify the order within 30 days after it is noted in the CCS (a belated motion needs good cause and a finding); responses are due in 15 days, and the motion is deemed denied if not set within 30 days or not ruled on within 30 days after hearing or filing. If certified, move the Court of Appeals to accept jurisdiction within 30 days after the certification is noted in the CCS (response in 15 days), then file the Notice of Appeal within 15 days after the acceptance order. Class-certification orders may go directly to the Court of Appeals by motion within 30 days. Grounds include substantial expense or injury from waiting, a substantial question of law whose early answer will promote orderly disposition, or an otherwise inadequate remedy.","c":"Ind. App. R. 14(B), (C)"},{"t":"Interlocutory appeals do not stay the case","d":"An interlocutory appeal does not stay trial-court proceedings unless the trial court or a Court of Appeals judge orders a stay, which may require a bond. Brief extensions in interlocutory appeals are disfavored, and a motion to shorten time is due within 10 days of the notice or acceptance motion.","c":"Ind. App. R. 14(G), (H)"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP: appeal for failure to rule","d":"Under the anti-SLAPP statute, if the trial court does not act on the motion to dismiss within 30 days after the submission of the evidence discovered in the expedited period, the movant may appeal based on the failure to rule. The statute itself gives no other interlocutory appeal.","c":"Ind. Code § 34-7-7-9(e)–(f)"},{"t":"Stays pending appeal","d":"An appeal does not stay the judgment unless a court orders it. You must first ask the trial court (or agency); the Court of Appeals considers a stay only after denial, a failure to rule within a reasonable time, or extraordinary circumstances, and the motion must include certified or verified copies of the order and ruling plus service and notice certificates. The court may set a bond under App.R. 18.","c":"Ind. App. R. 39(A)–(E)"}]],["Record and briefing",[{"t":"Clerk’s Record and Transcript","d":"The trial court clerk assembles the Clerk’s Record within 30 days after the Notice of Appeal and files a Notice of Completion of Clerk’s Record. The court reporter has 45 days after the Notice of Appeal to file the Transcript, and the clerk serves a Notice of Completion of Transcript within 5 days after it is filed. Within 10 days after the notice, a party must arrange payment of the balance of the transcript cost (a 50% deposit may be required). If a notice of completion is overdue, the appellant must seek an order compelling it no later than 7 days after it was due.","c":"Ind. App. R. 9(H), 10(B)–(G), 11(B)"},{"t":"Appendices filed with the briefs","d":"Each party files its own Appendix of the record parts needed for the appeal on or before the day its brief is filed; supplemental appendices are allowed without leave until the final reply brief. Leaving something out of an Appendix does not waive any issue.","c":"Ind. App. R. 49(A)–(B), 50"},{"t":"Briefing schedule","d":"Appellant’s brief: 30 days after service of the Notice of Completion of Clerk’s Record (if the Transcript is complete or none was requested) or otherwise of the Notice of Completion of Transcript; the 3-day mail extension does not apply to this deadline. Appellee’s brief: 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief. Reply: 15 days after the appellee’s brief (30 days if it is also the cross-appellee’s brief). Cross-appellant’s reply: 15 days after the appellant’s reply. A late appellant’s brief risks summary dismissal; a missing appellee’s brief allows reversal on a showing of prima facie error.","c":"Ind. App. R. 45(B), (D)"},{"t":"Length limits","d":"Without a word count, briefs are limited to 30 pages (appellant and appellee), 15 pages (reply; amicus), or 30 pages (reply plus cross-appellee). With a word-count certificate, the limits are 14,000 words (appellant, appellee, reply plus cross-appellee), 7,000 words (reply, amicus), and 4,200 words for petitions for rehearing or transfer and responses (1,000 words for a transfer reply). Headings and footnotes count. A motion for an oversized brief must be filed at least 15 days before the due date and request a number of words, not pages.","c":"Ind. App. R. 44(B)–(F)"},{"t":"Extensions of time","d":"A verified motion for extension must be filed at least 7 days before the deadline unless the movant was unaware of the facts, and never after the deadline; it must give the dates, the requested business-day due date, and the reason (identified conflicting litigation, complexity, or hardship). No extensions are allowed for rehearing or transfer petitions or responses, or in termination-of-parental-rights appeals; in worker’s compensation, custody, support, visitation, paternity, adoption, and CHINS appeals extensions require extraordinary circumstances.","c":"Ind. App. R. 35(A)–(D)"},{"t":"Computing time","d":"Exclude the trigger day; if the last day is a Saturday, Sunday, legal holiday, or a day the Clerk’s office is closed, the period runs to the next business day. For periods under 7 days, exclude all non-business days. Service by mail or commercial carrier adds 3 calendar days to responses, but not to rehearing or transfer deadlines or when service is by E-Service.","c":"Ind. App. R. 25(A)–(C)"},{"t":"Defective filings","d":"A non-compliant document is stamped ‘received’ but not filed, and the Clerk sends a Notice of Defect. Most filers have 10 business days to cure (20 for unrepresented incarcerated persons); a timely cure is deemed filed as of the corrected filing and treated as timely.","c":"Ind. App. R. 23(D)"}]],["After the Court of Appeals decides",[{"t":"Opinions and memorandum decisions","d":"Court of Appeals cases that do not meet the publication criteria are decided by memorandum decision. Memorandum decisions issued on or after January 1, 2023 may be cited for persuasive value; earlier ones may be cited only for res judicata, collateral estoppel, or law of the case. A party may move to publish within 15 days after the decision. Published Court of Appeals opinions bind all Indiana trial courts.","c":"Ind. App. R. 65(A), (B), (D)"},{"t":"Rehearing","d":"A petition for rehearing may be sought from a published opinion, a memorandum decision, an order dismissing an appeal, or an order declining to authorize a successive post-conviction petition, but not from an order denying transfer. It is due 30 days after the decision with no extensions and no 3-day mail extension. No response is required unless the court asks (response due 15 days after service or the request), and replies are prohibited.","c":"Ind. App. R. 54(A)–(D)"},{"t":"Certification of the decision","d":"The trial court and parties may not act in reliance on an appellate decision until it is certified, which happens only after the time for rehearing, transfer, or review expires (or after the Supreme Court finally disposes of a granted transfer), unless all parties ask for earlier certification.","c":"Ind. App. R. 65(E)"}]],["Indiana Supreme Court",[{"t":"Mandatory jurisdiction","d":"The Supreme Court has mandatory and exclusive jurisdiction over death-penalty appeals, appeals of final judgments declaring a state or federal statute unconstitutional in whole or part, parental-consent waiver appeals, and mandate-of-funds appeals. All other review is discretionary.","c":"Ind. App. R. 4(A)"},{"t":"Petition to transfer","d":"After an adverse Court of Appeals decision (opinion, memorandum decision, amendment of either, or dismissal order), file a petition to transfer within 45 days after the decision, or within 30 days after the ruling on rehearing if rehearing was sought, with a $125 fee (none for governmental or in forma pauperis parties). No extensions and no 3-day mail extension. Responses are due 20 days after service (or file a notice of no response, which must contain no argument), and a reply 10 days after the response. Orders denying interlocutory-appeal motions are not ‘adverse decisions’ that can be transferred.","c":"Ind. App. R. 56(B), 57(B)–(F)"},{"t":"What the Court looks for","d":"Transfer is discretionary. The listed considerations are a conflict among Court of Appeals decisions, a conflict with Supreme Court precedent, a conflict with U.S. Supreme Court or federal court of appeals decisions on an important federal question, an important undecided question of law or great public importance, Supreme Court precedent that needs reconsideration, and a significant departure from accepted law or practice. The petition starts with a non-argumentative Question Presented on its own page.","c":"Ind. App. R. 57(G)–(H)"},{"t":"Effect of granting or denying transfer","d":"If transfer is granted, the Court of Appeals decision is vacated except parts the Supreme Court expressly adopts or summarily affirms, and the Supreme Court takes the whole appeal. Denial ends the litigation in the Supreme Court and has no other legal effect; rehearing of a denial is not allowed. An evenly divided Court denies transfer, or, after granting, reinstates the Court of Appeals decision.","c":"Ind. App. R. 58(A)–(C)"},{"t":"Emergency transfer before the Court of Appeals rules","d":"In rare cases, on a verified motion showing a substantial question of law of great public importance and an emergency requiring speedy determination, the Supreme Court may take an appeal directly.","c":"Ind. App. R. 56(A)"}]],["E-filing",[{"t":"Mandatory e-filing in the appellate courts","d":"Attorneys must file electronically through the Indiana E-Filing System (IEFS) under a user agreement with an e-filing service provider; an exemption requires a motion in each case, granted only for good cause. A document is filed at the time on the Notice of Electronic Filing, subject to payment of fees, and must be completed before midnight in the time zone of the court where the case is pending. E-Service on public service contacts is complete on transmission. If the IEFS fails, a party may file conventionally.","c":"Ind. App. R. 23(A), 68(A), (C), (F), (I), (M)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of Appeal (final judgment)","d":"30 days from entry of the final judgment noted in the CCS.\nFiled with the appellate Clerk with a $250 fee. Untimely notice forfeits the appeal.","c":"Ind. App. R. 9(A)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the final judgment noted in the CCS)","note":"Filed with the appellate Clerk with a $250 fee. Untimely notice forfeits the appeal."}},{"t":"Motion to correct error","d":"30 days from entry of the final judgment noted in the CCS.\nCannot be extended (T.R. 6(C)). Opposing statement due 15 days after service.","c":"Ind. T.R. 59(C)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the final judgment noted in the CCS)","note":"Cannot be extended (T.R. 6(C)). Opposing statement due 15 days after service."}},{"t":"Notice of Appeal after a motion to correct error","d":"30 days from ruling noted in the CCS or deemed denial under T.R. 53.3, whichever is first.\nDeemed denied if not set within 45 days, or not ruled on within 30 days after hearing or 45 days after filing without a hearing.","c":"Ind. App. R. 9(A)(1); Ind. T.R. 53.3","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (ruling noted in the CCS or deemed denial under T.R. 53.3, whichever is first)","note":"Deemed denied if not set within 45 days, or not ruled on within 30 days after hearing or 45 days after filing without a hearing."}},{"t":"Notice of Appeal (agency judicial review in the Court of Appeals)","d":"30 days from date of the agency order, ruling, or decision.\nApplies notwithstanding any statute to the contrary.","c":"Ind. App. R. 9(A)(3)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (date of the agency order, ruling, or decision)","note":"Applies notwithstanding any statute to the contrary."}},{"t":"Interlocutory appeal of right","d":"30 days from notation of the interlocutory order in the CCS.\nIncludes orders for the payment of money and preliminary injunctions.","c":"Ind. App. R. 14(A)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (notation of the interlocutory order in the CCS)","note":"Includes orders for the payment of money and preliminary injunctions."}},{"t":"Motion to certify interlocutory order (trial court)","d":"30 days from notation of the order in the CCS.\nResponse due 15 days after service; deemed denied if the court does not act within 30 days.","c":"Ind. App. R. 14(B)(1)(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (notation of the order in the CCS)","note":"Response due 15 days after service; deemed denied if the court does not act within 30 days."}},{"t":"Motion asking the Court of Appeals to accept interlocutory appeal","d":"30 days from notation of the trial court’s certification in the CCS.\nResponse due 15 days after service.","c":"Ind. App. R. 14(B)(2)(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (notation of the trial court’s certification in the CCS)","note":"Response due 15 days after service."}},{"t":"Notice of Appeal after acceptance of discretionary interlocutory appeal","d":"15 days from court of Appeals order accepting jurisdiction.","c":"Ind. App. R. 14(B)(3), (C)(5)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (court of Appeals order accepting jurisdiction)","note":""}},{"t":"Class-certification interlocutory appeal motion","d":"30 days from notation of the class-certification order in the CCS.","c":"Ind. App. R. 14(C)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (notation of the class-certification order in the CCS)","note":""}},{"t":"Transcript payment agreement with the court reporter","d":"10 days from filing of the Notice of Appeal.\nCourt reporter may require a 50% deposit.","c":"Ind. App. R. 9(H)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the Notice of Appeal)","note":"Court reporter may require a 50% deposit."}},{"t":"Clerk’s Record assembled","d":"30 days from filing of the Notice of Appeal.","c":"Ind. App. R. 10(B)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the Notice of Appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Transcript filed by court reporter","d":"45 days from filing of the Notice of Appeal.\nExtensions by the reporter’s motion under App.R. 35(A).","c":"Ind. App. R. 11(B)","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the Notice of Appeal)","note":"Extensions by the reporter’s motion under App.R. 35(A)."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"30 days from service of the Notice of Completion of Clerk’s Record (if Transcript complete or not requested), otherwise of the Notice of Completion of Transcript.\nThe 3-day mail extension does not apply. Appendix due the same day (App.R. 49(A)).","c":"Ind. App. R. 45(B)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the Notice of Completion of Clerk’s Record)","note":"The 3-day mail extension does not apply. Appendix due the same day (App.R. 49(A))."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.","c":"Ind. App. R. 45(B)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"15 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\n30 days if it is also the cross-appellee’s brief.","c":"Ind. App. R. 45(B)(3)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellee’s brief)","note":"30 days if it is also the cross-appellee’s brief."}},{"t":"Cross-appellant’s reply brief","d":"15 days from service of the appellant’s reply brief.","c":"Ind. App. R. 45(B)(4)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s reply brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion for extension of time","d":"7 days from before the deadline expires (at least 7 days before).\nNever after the deadline; none for rehearing or transfer.","c":"Ind. App. R. 35(A)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (before the deadline expires)","note":"Never after the deadline; none for rehearing or transfer."}},{"t":"Motion for oversized brief","d":"15 days from before the brief is due (at least 15 days before).\nState words requested, not pages.","c":"Ind. App. R. 44(B)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (before the brief is due)","note":"State words requested, not pages."}},{"t":"Motion to publish a memorandum decision","d":"15 days from entry of the decision.","c":"Ind. App. R. 65(B)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the decision)","note":""}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"30 days from the decision.\nNo extensions; mail extension does not apply.","c":"Ind. App. R. 54(B)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (the decision)","note":"No extensions; mail extension does not apply."}},{"t":"Petition to transfer (no rehearing sought)","d":"45 days from the adverse Court of Appeals decision.\n$125 fee (App.R. 56(B)). No extensions.","c":"Ind. App. R. 57(C)(1)","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (the adverse Court of Appeals decision)","note":"$125 fee (App.R. 56(B)). No extensions."}},{"t":"Petition to transfer (after rehearing)","d":"30 days from court of Appeals disposition of the petition for rehearing.\nNo extensions.","c":"Ind. App. R. 57(C)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (court of Appeals disposition of the petition for rehearing)","note":"No extensions."}},{"t":"Response to petition to transfer","d":"20 days from service of the petition.\nMail extension may apply; no other extension.","c":"Ind. App. R. 57(D)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (service of the petition)","note":"Mail extension may apply; no other extension."}},{"t":"Reply on transfer","d":"10 days from service of the response.","c":"Ind. App. R. 57(E)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the response)","note":""}},{"t":"Cure of a Notice of Defect","d":"10 days from date of the Notice of Defect (business days).\n20 business days for unrepresented incarcerated persons.","c":"Ind. App. R. 23(D)(1)(a)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (date of the Notice of Defect)","note":"20 business days for unrepresented incarcerated persons."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of Appeal (final judgment)",30,"Entry of the final judgment noted in the CCS","Ind. App. R. 9(A)(1)","Filed with the appellate Clerk with a $250 fee. Untimely notice forfeits the appeal."],["Motion to correct error",30,"Entry of the final judgment noted in the CCS","Ind. T.R. 59(C)","Cannot be extended (T.R. 6(C)). Opposing statement due 15 days after service."],["Notice of Appeal after a motion to correct error",30,"Ruling noted in the CCS or deemed denial under T.R. 53.3, whichever is first","Ind. App. R. 9(A)(1); Ind. T.R. 53.3","Deemed denied if not set within 45 days, or not ruled on within 30 days after hearing or 45 days after filing without a hearing."],["Notice of Appeal (agency judicial review in the Court of Appeals)",30,"Date of the agency order, ruling, or decision","Ind. App. R. 9(A)(3)","Applies notwithstanding any statute to the contrary."],["Interlocutory appeal of right",30,"Notation of the interlocutory order in the CCS","Ind. App. R. 14(A)","Includes orders for the payment of money and preliminary injunctions."],["Motion to certify interlocutory order (trial court)",30,"Notation of the order in the CCS","Ind. App. R. 14(B)(1)(a)","Response due 15 days after service; deemed denied if the court does not act within 30 days."],["Motion asking the Court of Appeals to accept interlocutory appeal",30,"Notation of the trial court’s certification in the CCS","Ind. App. R. 14(B)(2)(a)","Response due 15 days after service."],["Notice of Appeal after acceptance of discretionary interlocutory appeal",15,"Court of Appeals order accepting jurisdiction","Ind. App. R. 14(B)(3), (C)(5)",""],["Class-certification interlocutory appeal motion",30,"Notation of the class-certification order in the CCS","Ind. App. R. 14(C)(1)",""],["Transcript payment agreement with the court reporter",10,"Filing of the Notice of Appeal","Ind. App. R. 9(H)","Court reporter may require a 50% deposit."],["Clerk’s Record assembled",30,"Filing of the Notice of Appeal","Ind. App. R. 10(B)",""],["Transcript filed by court reporter",45,"Filing of the Notice of Appeal","Ind. App. R. 11(B)","Extensions by the reporter’s motion under App.R. 35(A)."],["Appellant’s brief",30,"Service of the Notice of Completion of Clerk’s Record (if Transcript complete or not requested), otherwise of the Notice of Completion of Transcript","Ind. App. R. 45(B)(1)","The 3-day mail extension does not apply. Appendix due the same day (App.R. 49(A))."],["Appellee’s brief",30,"Service of the appellant’s brief","Ind. App. R. 45(B)(2)",""],["Reply brief",15,"Service of the appellee’s brief","Ind. App. R. 45(B)(3)","30 days if it is also the cross-appellee’s brief."],["Cross-appellant’s reply brief",15,"Service of the appellant’s reply brief","Ind. App. R. 45(B)(4)",""],["Motion for extension of time",7,"Before the deadline expires (at least 7 days before)","Ind. App. R. 35(A)","Never after the deadline; none for rehearing or transfer."],["Motion for oversized brief",15,"Before the brief is due (at least 15 days before)","Ind. App. R. 44(B)","State words requested, not pages."],["Motion to publish a memorandum decision",15,"Entry of the decision","Ind. App. R. 65(B)",""],["Petition for rehearing",30,"The decision","Ind. App. R. 54(B)","No extensions; mail extension does not apply."],["Petition to transfer (no rehearing sought)",45,"The adverse Court of Appeals decision","Ind. App. R. 57(C)(1)","$125 fee (App.R. 56(B)). No extensions."],["Petition to transfer (after rehearing)",30,"Court of Appeals disposition of the petition for rehearing","Ind. App. R. 57(C)(2)","No extensions."],["Response to petition to transfer",20,"Service of the petition","Ind. App. R. 57(D)","Mail extension may apply; no other extension."],["Reply on transfer",10,"Service of the response","Ind. App. R. 57(E)",""],["Cure of a Notice of Defect",10,"Date of the Notice of Defect (business days)","Ind. App. R. 23(D)(1)(a)","20 business days for unrepresented incarcerated persons."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.in.gov/courts/"]]},"rulesName":"Indiana Rules of Trial Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of the rules","s":"The rules govern procedure in all Indiana courts in all civil suits, whether at law, in equity, or of statutory origin, and must be construed to secure the just, speedy and inexpensive determination of every action. Courts may adopt local and administrative district rules consistent with Rule 81.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the “civil action.” A civil action is not merged in a public offense or public remedy and may be pursued independently of, and in addition to, any criminal punishment or public relief.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of an action","s":"An action is commenced by filing a complaint (or equivalent pleading required by statute), paying the filing fee or filing an order waiving it, and, where service is required, furnishing the clerk enough copies of the complaint and summons. Unlike FRCP 3, payment of the fee (or a waiver) and delivery of service copies to the clerk are part of commencement.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"3.1","t":"Appearance","s":"Indiana requires an appearance form, with no FRCP counterpart: the initiating party (or counsel) files it when the action is commenced, responding parties when they first appear, and intervenors with their first filing, listing contact information, attorney number, case type, whether service by FAX or e-mail is accepted, related cases, and other items (Social Security numbers of family members in support-related cases, identifiers in mental health commitments other than 72-hour emergency detentions, a public mailing address in protection-order cases, and a confidential Guardianship Information Sheet in guardianship cases). Information must be supplemented and changes reported promptly, and the clerk uses the form for service under T.R. 5(B). An attorney may move to withdraw only after sending the client written notice of intent at least 10 days before filing (not required in criminal, family law, and juvenile cases with nothing pending or set, or after other counsel appears), and the motion must be granted unless the court finds withdrawal unreasonable; the rule also provides for notices of temporary or limited representation and for substitution of appearance signed by both attorneys.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4","t":"Process","s":"The court gets jurisdiction over a person who commences or joins the action, is served with summons, enters an appearance, or is otherwise subject to its power. When the complaint is filed, the party furnishes copies of the complaint and summons to the clerk, who signs, seals and issues the summons (with any praecipe); the summons must state the response time and warn of default. The party may designate the manner of service, but if none is designated the clerk serves by mail or other public means, with sheriff service if no address is known or mail is not accepted; the summons and complaint are served together (but the complaint is not published), and service by publication is deemed made at the end of the day of last required publication.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.1","t":"Summons: service on individuals","s":"Service on an individual (including one acting in a representative capacity) is by (1) registered or certified mail or other public means with a return receipt requested and returned, sent to the residence, place of business or employment, (2) delivering a copy personally, (3) leaving a copy at the dwelling house or usual place of abode, or (4) serving an agent under a rule, statute or valid agreement. When copy is left at the abode or served on an agent, the server must also mail a copy by first class mail to the address on the summons and note this on the return. If a mailed copy is returned before judgment or within 30 days after judgment, the party relying on that service must notify the court.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.2","t":"Summons: service upon infant or incompetents","s":"Service on an infant is made on the next friend or guardian ad litem in the same action, otherwise on a court-appointed representative who can be served in Indiana, otherwise on a parent with custody or a person standing in place of a parent; the infant must also be served if 14 or older, and is served directly if none of these is possible. Service on an incompetent follows the same order, except that absent a representative it is made on both the named party and the person with custody. An appearance by a guardian, next friend, guardian ad litem or their attorney cures service defects unless challenged.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.3","t":"Summons: service upon institutionalized persons","s":"Service on a person imprisoned or restrained in an institution is made by delivering or mailing the summons and complaint to the official in charge. The official must immediately deliver the papers to the person, allow the person to arrange counsel, and state on the return whether the person received the summons and had an opportunity to retain counsel.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.4","t":"Service upon persons in actions for acts done in this state or having an effect in this state","s":"Indiana’s long-arm rule subjects nonresidents, departed residents, and persons of unknown residence to jurisdiction for claims arising from eight listed acts (including doing business in Indiana, causing injury by acts in or affecting Indiana, owning Indiana real property, insuring Indiana risks, living in a marital relationship in Indiana for support and property obligations, and abuse or protective-order violations affecting someone in Indiana), and also on any basis not inconsistent with the Indiana or U.S. Constitutions. Such persons may be served under Rules 4.1, 4.5, 4.6 or 4.9, or through the Secretary of State as their deemed agent under Rule 4.10. The court may send the case to a more convenient forum, but no forum non conveniens stay or dismissal may be granted until all properly joined defendants file a stipulation to submit to jurisdiction in the other forum and waive any statute of limitations defense there; the order must be withdrawn if a stipulation is violated.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.5","t":"Summons: service upon resident who cannot be found or served within the state","s":"When an Indiana resident cannot be served personally or by agent in Indiana and cannot be found, has concealed their whereabouts, or has left the state, summons may be served in the manner provided for in rem actions under Rule 4.9.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.6","t":"Service upon organizations","s":"Service on an organization is made on an executive officer or an appointed or statutory agent; on a partnership, a general partner; on a state governmental organization, its executive officer and also the Attorney General; and on a local governmental organization, its executive and its attorney (plus the representative personally when named individually). Service is made in the manner used for individuals but must not knowingly be directed to the person’s residence unless that address is furnished under a statute or agreement or an affidavit shows other service is impractical; a registered agent that consented to electronic service may be served electronically. If service under (A) or (B) cannot be made, as shown by affidavit or return, a copy may be left with the person in charge of any Indiana office of the organization.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.7","t":"Summons: service upon agent named by statute or agreement","s":"When an agent is designated by statute or valid agreement to receive service, a governmental agent is served under Rule 4.10 and any other agent under Rule 4.1 or 4.6. If the agent cannot be served because no address was furnished or the agent’s whereabouts in Indiana are unknown, the principal is deemed to have appointed the Secretary of State, who may be served under Rule 4.10.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.8","t":"Summons: service of pleadings or summons on Attorney General","s":"Service of a summons, complaint or any pleading on the Attorney General is made personally on the Attorney General, a deputy or a clerk at the office, or by mail or other public means to that office under Rule 4.1(A)(1) and, as applicable, Rule 4.11.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.9","t":"Summons: in rem actions","s":"In an action involving a res located in Indiana, the court may enter judgment to the extent of its jurisdiction over the res. Service may be made by summons on the person or agent under these rules, by service outside Indiana under Rule 4.1 or other rules, or by publication under Rule 4.13 (inside or outside Indiana).","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.10","t":"Summons: service upon Secretary of State or other governmental agent","s":"When the Secretary of State or another governmental officer is served as agent, the party must request that service in the praecipe, state the person’s recorded or last known address (or that none is known), and pay any statutory fee, and the clerk forwards copies to the agent. The agent must promptly send the summons and complaint by registered or certified mail or other public means with written acknowledgment of receipt, file an affidavit with the clerk showing the mailing date (or why there was no mailing), send the clerk the return receipt and summons, and keep a copy of the receipt. The rule contains no subdivision (B).","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.11","t":"Summons: registered or certified mail","s":"When service by registered or certified mail or other public means with a return receipt is authorized, the clerk (or a Rule 4.10 governmental agent) mails the summons and complaint to the address on the summons or supplied by the party. The return shows the date and place of mailing and the return receipt, including whether and by whom it was accepted, and is filed as part of the record; if the clerk’s mailing comes back unaccepted, the clerk reissues the summons for service as the party requests.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.12","t":"Summons: service by sheriff or other officer","s":"Personal and abode service under Rule 4.1 is made by the sheriff, a deputy, or a person appointed by the court; service by an unauthorized person is still effective, but that person must prove it as a witness or by deposition with no expenses taxed as costs. A sheriff, deputy, or full-time state or municipal police officer may agree to serve summons in any Indiana county (but not by agreement with the sheriff in the sheriff’s own county), and agreed expenses are never recoverable as costs; summons may be issued for service in any county. Service outside Indiana may be made by any disinterested person or the party’s attorney, with expenses taxable only if reasonable and mail service could not be made or failed.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.13","t":"Summons: service by publication","s":"Where publication is permitted, the party requests it in the praecipe with affidavits of diligent search showing the defendant cannot be found, has concealed their whereabouts, or has left the state, and one summons may name all persons served. The summons must describe the suit and any res and state that the defendant must respond within 30 days after the last publication or face default. It is published 3 times, the first promptly and each of the next 2 at least 7 and not more than 14 days after the prior one, in a qualified newspaper in the county of filing, of the res, or of the defendant’s residence or last known residence (or the nearest county with a paper), and the return includes the printer’s affidavit and publication dates.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.14","t":"Service under special order of court","s":"On a verified motion showing that prior attempts at service under the rules have failed, the court may order service in any other manner reasonably calculated to give the defendant actual knowledge of the proceedings and an opportunity to be heard. The rule expressly allows social media, email, or other technology.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.15","t":"Summons: proof of service—return—amendments—defects","s":"The server must promptly sign and deliver a return to the clerk stating the time, place and manner of service or, if service failed, how it was thwarted; the return, praecipe and affidavits become part of the record, and the clerk’s filing and issuance dates are evidence without further authentication. A signed written admission of service stating the date and place is evidence of proper service. The court may allow amendment of process or proof of service unless material prejudice would result, and no summons or service may be set aside if it is reasonably calculated to inform the person of the action, the court, and the time to respond.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.16","t":"Summons: duties of persons to aid in service","s":"Everyone being served must cooperate, accept service, and acknowledge personal service in writing; offering or tendering the papers and telling the person they are being served is adequate, and a person who refuses tender may not later challenge that service. A person who accepts service for another must promptly deliver the papers or notify that person, or within a reasonable time notify the clerk or server in writing that delivery was not possible. No one through whom service is made may penalize or discriminate against the person served, and willful violations may be punished by contempt.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"4.17","t":"Summons: certain proceedings excepted","s":"Rules 4 through 4.16 do not replace special statutory or rule-based methods of service or notice in proceedings such as decedents’ estates, guardianships, receiverships, and assignments for the benefit of creditors.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings, documents, and other papers","s":"Every party and any special judge must be served with later pleadings, written non-ex parte motions, briefs, discovery papers, notices, appearances, offers of judgment and similar papers; parties in default for failure to appear need not be served, except that new claims against them must be served as a summons under Rule 4. Service on a represented party is on the attorney, by delivery (refusing a tendered document waives objection to service), by mail or third-party commercial carrier (complete on mailing), or by IOJA-approved electronic means to the fax or email on the appearance form, which is complete on transmission unless sent on a weekend, legal holiday, court-closed day, or after 5:00 p.m. recipient’s local time, in which case it is complete the next business day. A certificate of service must appear at the end of every filed document; papers must be filed before or within a reasonable time after service, but discovery requests, responses and depositions under Rules 27, 30, 31, 33, 34 and 36 are not filed unless needed for a Rule 26(C) or 37 motion or ordered filed for use, and filing a deposition constitutes publication; filing may be made by delivery to the clerk, electronic filing, registered, certified or express mail, or a third-party commercial carrier for delivery within 3 calendar days (mail and carrier filing complete on deposit), and the clerk distributes signed orders to non-defaulting parties without an email address on file, except that in Rule 69 proceedings the party who submitted the proposed order distributes it.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Periods exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, legal holiday, or day the office is closed, and for periods of less than 7 days intermediate weekends, holidays and closed days are excluded. A party may get one automatic 30-day enlargement to respond to a complaint or other pleading by filing a notice, on or before the original due date, stating the original and enlarged due dates, with no order needed; other enlargements require a motion (for cause before the deadline, or for excusable neglect after it), and time under Rules 50(A), 52(B), 56, 59(C), 59(E) and 60(B) cannot be extended except as those rules allow. Unless otherwise provided, a response to a pleading is due 20 days after service, a response to a motion 20 days after service, and a reply 14 days after the response (not applicable to continuance motions, Rule 56, Rule 59 or Rule 60(B) motions); written motions and hearing notices must be served at least 5 days before the hearing, opposing affidavits at least 1 day before, 3 days are added after service by U.S. mail, and a dissolution or legal separation case cannot be heard until 60 days after filing the petition or first publication to a nonresident.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings and motions","s":"The only pleadings are a complaint and answer, a counterclaim and reply, a cross-claim and answer, and a third-party complaint and answer. Applications for orders must be by written motion (unless made at a hearing or trial) stating the grounds and relief and accompanied by a separate proposed order, and a hearing must be requested by separate written motion. A motion for continuance must be filed as soon as the cause is discovered, must state whether the opponent objects, does not object, or has an unknown position (with the date, time and method of the attempt to confer), and must estimate the delay needed and the length of the rescheduled hearing or trial; local rules on continuances are abrogated.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim requires a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for relief, but a complaint for personal injury, death, or punitive damages may not state any dollar amount. Denials may be general (subject to Rules 11 and 11.1) or specific, a lack of knowledge operates as a denial, and averments other than damages are admitted if not denied. Indiana’s list of affirmative defenses, which the pleader must raise and prove, adds to the federal list items such as lack of subject matter or personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficiency of process or service, and the same action pending in another Indiana state court; pleadings addressed to or by multiple parties or paragraphs are construed as joint, separate and several.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity, authority, and legal existence need not be averred, and lack of them must be pleaded as an affirmative defense by the person asserting it. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with specificity while conditions of mind may be averred generally; performance of conditions precedent may be averred generally, but a denial must be specific and particular. Special damages must be specifically stated, and a default judgment cannot exceed the relief demanded in the complaint.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"9.1","t":"Pleading and proof of contributory negligence, assumed risk, res ipsa loquitur, consideration, bona fide purchaser, matters of judicial notice—answer of distraint","s":"The defendant bears the burden of pleading and proving contributory negligence, assumption of risk or incurred risk, and may plead them by denial. Res ipsa loquitur may be pleaded generally; lack of consideration for a written contract or release is an affirmative defense; and bona fide purchaser status must be pleaded and proved by the party asserting it, though once value and any recording or perfection are shown, good faith is found unless contrary evidence is introduced. Presumptions of law and judicially noticeable matters need not be pleaded, and the rule provides a sufficient answer in actions over property distrained while doing damage.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"9.2","t":"Pleading and proof of written instruments","s":"A pleading founded on a written instrument must include or attach the original or a copy; a claim on a written contract must attach a copy (unless not in the plaintiff’s custody), and a claim on an account must attach an Affidavit of Debt substantially like Appendix A-2. A non-original creditor suing on a consumer debt (other than a mortgage foreclosure) must also attach the signed original contract (or documents generated when the debt was incurred or the card used), a chronological list of all prior owners with transfer dates, and an authenticated bill of sale to the plaintiff. Execution of an attached instrument is deemed established and the instrument admitted unless execution is denied under oath on personal knowledge, the pleader must allow inspection of the original on request, and noncompliance may be raised in the first responsive pleading or prior motion and corrected by amendment under Rule 15; these requirements do not run against deceased, infant, incompetent, or insolvent parties or their representatives.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings, motions, memoranda, and briefs","s":"Counsel’s filings must be on 8 1/2 by 11 inch pages (white paper, one side only, when filed conventionally), in black type, using one of the listed fonts at 12-point or larger in text and footnotes, double-spaced (with single-spaced footnotes, tables and block quotes separated by at least 4-point spaces), numbered consecutively at the bottom starting with 1, with margins of at least one inch. Every pleading needs a caption with the court, title, file number and Rule 7(A) designation (the complaint naming all parties), and averments must be in numbered paragraphs; self-represented litigants must file neat, legible, one-sided documents and comply with the caption and paragraph rules. The court may accept substantially compliant filings and must allow a reasonable time to cure nonconforming ones.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"11","t":"Parties represented by an attorney: signing, certification, and verification","s":"Every submission by a represented party must be signed by at least one attorney of record with address, telephone number and attorney number, and need not be verified unless a rule requires. The signature certifies that the attorney read the filing, that factual contentions and denials have or will likely have evidentiary support, that legal contentions are warranted by existing law or a nonfrivolous argument, that any cited legal authority is authentic, and that the filing is not for delay or harassment; the court may strike noncompliant filings and scandalous matter, and the rule expressly covers filings prepared with artificial intelligence or other resources. Verification may be made by affirming under penalties for perjury in the stated form, and affidavits on summary judgment and in denial of execution under Rule 9.2 must be on personal knowledge.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"11.1","t":"Parties not represented by an attorney: signing, certification, and verification","s":"A self-represented party must sign every submission and provide a mailing address, telephone number, and email address if available, except information protected by the Rules on Access to Court Records or replaceable by a public mailing address. The signature carries the same certifications as Rule 11, including that cited authority is authentic, and applies to filings prepared with artificial intelligence or other resources. The court may strike noncompliant filings and scandalous matter, and Rules 11(D) and 11(E) on verification apply.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"11.2","t":"Abusive litigation","s":"On motion or its own initiative, and after considering the filer’s litigation history, the court may find a filer to be an abusive litigant, based on conduct such as frivolous or repetitive claims, bad-faith litigation, citing fictitious legal authority, or egregious rule violations. The court may then impose tailored restrictions, including requiring sworn affidavits with filings, lists of prior related cases, explanations why res judicata, collateral estoppel or law of the case does not bar new actions, limits on reconsideration motions, page, word, title and exhibit limits, and denial of filing-fee waivers for barred or meritless claims. After an opportunity for hearing and a finding of bad faith or failed remedies, the court may sanction the filer or attorney with default, dismissal with prejudice, reasonable expenses, and reasonable attorney’s fees.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses, objections, and motions on the pleadings","s":"Defenses may be made by motion for lack of subject matter or personal jurisdiction, incorrect venue under Rule 75, insufficiency of process or service, failure to state a claim (including failure to name the real party in interest), failure to join a party under Rule 19, and, unlike the FRCP, the same action pending in another Indiana state court; such a motion must be made before pleading or within 20 days after service if no responsive pleading is required. If the motion is denied, the responsive pleading is due within 10 days of the order; if granted with leave to correct, the amended pleading is due within 10 days and the response 10 days after that, and after dismissal under (B)(6) the pleading may be amended once as of right within 10 days after notice of the order. A motion to strike is due before responding or within 20 days after service if no response is permitted; a granted motion for more definite statement requires a corrective pleading within 10 days; matters outside the pleadings convert (B)(6) and judgment-on-the-pleadings motions to summary judgment; and personal jurisdiction, venue, process, service and same-action-pending defenses are waived if omitted from a Rule 12 motion or the responsive pleading.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and cross-claim","s":"A pleading must state as a counterclaim any claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence, unless it was already pending in another action or the opposing party sued by attachment without personal jurisdiction, and may state any other claim as a permissive counterclaim, which may exceed or differ from the opposing claim; cross-claims may be asserted against any co-party. After-acquired claims may be added by supplemental pleading with permission, omitted counterclaims by leave of court, and a statute of limitations or nonclaim statute does not bar a counterclaim to the extent it diminishes or defeats a same-transaction claim or relates to payment of or security for the other. The rule adds detailed Indiana provisions on counterclaims by and against transferees, successors, fiduciaries and sureties, substantive-law limits such as unfair priority in insolvency, and satisfaction of judgments by credits.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may bring in a nonparty who is or may be liable to it for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim, but must file the third-party complaint with its original answer or later by leave of court for good cause. The third-party defendant may assert defenses, counterclaims and cross-claims against the third-party plaintiff, other parties and the plaintiff, the plaintiff may assert claims against it, and a plaintiff facing a counterclaim may implead on the same terms. Any party may move for severance or a separate trial of a third-party claim with or before its responsive pleading.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as a matter of course before a responsive pleading is served or, if no response is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar, within 30 days after service; otherwise amendment requires leave (freely given when justice requires) or written consent, and a response to an amended pleading is due within the time remaining on the original or 20 days after service, whichever is longer. Issues tried by consent are treated as pleaded, and amendments to conform to the evidence may be made at any time, even after judgment. An amendment relates back if it arises from the same conduct, transaction or occurrence, and an amendment changing the defendant relates back if, within 120 days of commencement, the new party had notice and knew or should have known of the mistake in identity (with special notice rules for state and local governmental organizations); supplemental pleadings may be allowed on motion.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"16","t":"Pre-trial procedure: formulating issues","s":"In any non-criminal case the court may, and on any party’s motion must, hold a pre-trial conference on issues such as simplification, amendments, admissions, limits on expert witnesses, witness exchange, discovery orders, alternative dispute resolution, and dispositive motion deadlines. The clerk gives at least 30 days’ notice, at least 1 trial attorney per party must attend, and at least 10 days before the conference counsel must meet to mark and exchange exhibits (with a “P/T” prefix), prepare exhibit and fact stipulations, exchange witness lists, and discuss court-records access issues, discovery and settlement; a list of facts or exhibits the other side refused to stipulate to must be furnished at least 2 days before the conference, and later-discovered witnesses or exhibits must be disclosed immediately. The pre-trial order controls unless modified to prevent manifest injustice, and failure to appear or gross unpreparedness may result in an award of reasonable expenses and attorney’s fees or other appropriate action.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties plaintiff and defendant—capacity","s":"Actions must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, though executors, guardians, trustees and similar representatives may sue in their own names, and no dismissal may occur until a reasonable time after objection has been allowed for ratification, joinder or substitution. Capacity is governed by Indiana law, and partnerships and unincorporated associations may sue or be sued in their common names, but a money judgment against them binds an individual partner or member only if named or bound as a class member. Infants and incompetents may sue or be sued in their own names, through a guardian ad litem or next friend, or through a fiduciary representative, and the court must appoint a guardian ad litem if they are unrepresented; unknown persons may be named as unknown parties and their true names added by amendment at any time.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"17.1","t":"Parties: state as party—Attorney General","s":"When a pleading in a real property action (such as foreclosure, sale, partition, or quiet title) shows the State of Indiana claims a lien or interest, the state may be made a defendant, is bound by the judgment, and is served through the Attorney General under Rule 4.8. The Attorney General must defend and may designate the local prosecuting attorney as deputy, after which Rule 5 service goes to that prosecutor, and the state may appeal like other parties. In any case where the Attorney General represents the state, the presiding judge must promptly notify the Attorney General’s Indianapolis office by U.S. mail of any ruling or trial setting.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"A party asserting any claim may join as many legal, equitable, or statutory claims, independent or alternative, as it has against an opposing party. Claims that once had to await the outcome of another claim may be joined in one action, and a plaintiff may seek money and to set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first obtaining a money judgment.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"19","t":"Joinder of person needed for just adjudication","s":"A person subject to service must be joined if complete relief cannot be given without them or if their absence may impair their interest or expose existing parties to inconsistent obligations, and the court must order joinder (a refusing plaintiff may be made a defendant). If joinder is not made, the court weighs prejudice, ways to lessen it, adequacy of a judgment, and the plaintiff’s alternative remedies to decide whether to proceed or dismiss; nonjoinder is raised under Rule 12(B)(7). Indiana adds that joint obligors, assignors, and subrogors or subrogees are not indispensable, and that suits involving governmental organizations or officials in their official capacity are brought in the organization’s name and bind successors without substitution.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Persons may join as plaintiffs, or be joined as defendants, when the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence or series and share a common question of law or fact, and judgment may be given for or against fewer than all. Unwilling plaintiffs may be joined as defendants, and a defendant may bring in anyone who could be joined by alleging their interest. The court may order separate trials or other protective orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, expense or prejudice.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and non-joinder of parties; venue and jurisdiction over the subject-matter","s":"Misjoinder or non-joinder is not grounds for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties at any stage on just terms, sever claims, and allow misnomers to be corrected under Rule 15 at any time. If the court has venue or authority over any claim between the parties, it has venue and authority over all claims and persons joined, impleaded, or brought in by intervention, interpleader, counterclaim or cross-claim. It may transfer the case if venue depends on a sham or bad-faith claim, and may dismiss or consolidate as to a party against whom the same claim is already pending in another Indiana action.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"A plaintiff or defendant exposed to double or multiple liability may require adverse claimants to interplead even if their claims lack a common origin or the party denies liability, and the rule extends but does not reduce statutory interpleader. The complaint or answer must admit or describe the asserted liability, allege the risk of multiple liability, and pray that claimants litigate among themselves; it may show a deposit or bond and seek injunctive relief, claimants plead as if under Rule 13 within Rule 6 times, and defaulting parties need not be re-served with responsive pleadings under Rule 5(A) unless ordered. The party may deposit the money or property with the court and be discharged from liability.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"A class action requires numerosity, common questions, typicality, and adequate representation, plus one of the three familiar categories (risk of inconsistent or dispositive individual adjudications, generally applicable injunctive or declaratory relief, or predominance and superiority). The court decides certification as soon as practicable, (B)(3) members must receive the best practicable notice and may opt out, the court may manage the case by order and must allow reasonable attorney’s fees and expenses from a common fund, and dismissal or compromise requires court approval and notice. Unlike the FRCP, any judgment or settlement with a claims process must provide for residual funds, and not less than 50% of remaining residual funds must go to the Indiana Bar Foundation for the Coalition for Court Access, with the balance to the Foundation or other entities related to the litigation’s objectives or class interests.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions by shareholders","s":"A shareholder or member derivative complaint must be verified and allege that the plaintiff held the interest at the time of the transaction (or acquired it by operation of law) and, with particularity, the efforts made to obtain action from the directors or comparable authority and why they failed or were not made. The action cannot proceed if the plaintiff does not fairly and adequately represent similarly situated holders, and it may not be dismissed or compromised without court approval and notice as the court directs.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"Besides suing or being sued in its common name under Rule 17(E), an unincorporated association’s members may be sued as a class through named representative members if they will fairly and adequately protect the association’s and members’ interests. The court may issue orders like those under Rule 23(D), and dismissal or compromise follows Rule 23(E).","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"On timely motion, intervention is of right when a statute gives an unconditional right or the applicant has an interest in the property, fund or transaction that may be impaired and is not adequately represented. Permissive intervention is allowed when a statute gives a conditional right, the applicant’s claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact, or a governmental unit administers a statute or order relied on by a party, considering undue delay or prejudice. The motion is served under Rule 5 with the claim or defense, intervention after trial or judgment for Rule 50, 59 or 60 motions or appeal may be allowed, and the ruling is interlocutory unless made final under Rule 54(B).","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"If a party dies and the claim survives, the court may order substitution on motion by the court, any party, or the decedent’s successors or representatives, served on parties under Rule 5 and on nonparties as a summons under Rule 4, before or after judgment, with no fixed deadline stated in the rule; if the right survives only to or against remaining parties, the death is suggested on the record and the case proceeds. The same procedure applies to incompetency, a transferred interest may continue in the original party’s name unless the court orders substitution or joinder, and the substituted party may be a successor in interest or the estate’s representative (or the estate’s successor if the estate is closed or unnecessary). A judgment claim is allowed against the estate without filing if the representative is substituted within the probate claim period, no execution issues against a decedent’s property after death (existing liens may still be enforced), and a public officer’s successor is automatically substituted and may be named by official title.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Discovery may be had by depositions, interrogatories, production and entry requests, physical and mental examinations, and requests for admission, covering any non-privileged matter relevant to any party’s claim or defense (inadmissible information is discoverable if reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence), and the court must limit cumulative or overly burdensome discovery; unlike the FRCP, the rule has no mandatory initial disclosures or discovery-planning conference. Parties serving or answering interrogatories, production requests, or requests for admission must also serve them in an editable word-processing format, or a verified statement that they lack the equipment. Before filing any discovery motion, a party must make a reasonable effort to resolve the dispute and state in the motion the date, time, place, and participants of that effort, or the court may deny the motion.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A person expecting to be a party to an action may file a verified petition to perpetuate testimony, and must serve notice of the hearing on each expected adverse party at least 20 days before the hearing in the manner provided for a summons. While an appeal is pending, or before one is taken while the time has not expired, the trial court may allow depositions to perpetuate testimony for use in later proceedings. Filing of such depositions follows T.R. 5(E).","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken; discovery across state lines; before administrative agencies; and after judgment","s":"Depositions in the United States are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths or a court-appointed person, and abroad by notice, commission, or letter rogatory; the officer may not be a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or have a financial interest. Indiana courts may order discovery for use by tribunals outside the state. Parties to adjudicatory hearings before administrative agencies may use the discovery provisions of T.R. 26 through 37, seeking protective and other orders first from the agency, and discovery is allowed after judgment in proceedings to enforce or challenge it.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, the parties may by written stipulation provide that depositions be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice, and in any manner, and may modify the procedures for other discovery methods.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"Any party may depose any person after the action begins, but the plaintiff needs leave of court to take a deposition within 20 days after service of the summons and complaint, unless the defendant has already sought discovery or the plaintiff gives a signed special notice that the witness is leaving the state or will become unavailable. For an organization deposition, the parties must confer in good faith about the topics at least 15 days before the deposition, and the text sets no limit on the number or length of depositions. The witness must sign and return the transcript within 30 days after it is submitted, or it may be used as though signed.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"31","t":"Deposition of witnesses upon written questions","s":"Any party may depose any person on written questions served on all parties with a notice. Cross questions may be served within 20 days after the notice, redirect questions within 10 days after cross questions, and recross questions within 10 days after redirect questions, and the court may enlarge or shorten these times. The officer takes the answers and prepares and certifies the deposition as under T.R. 30.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"A deposition may be used to impeach the deponent, and a party’s (or a party organization’s officer’s or designee’s) deposition may be used by an adverse party for any purpose. Any witness’s deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, outside the state (unlike the FRCP’s 100-mile test), unable to attend due to age, sickness, infirmity, or imprisonment, cannot be subpoenaed, in exceptional circumstances, or by agreement. Objections to the form of written questions under T.R. 31 are waived unless served within the time for the next round of questions and within 5 days after service of the last questions.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Any party may serve interrogatories on another party, on a defendant with or after the summons and complaint, and the text sets no numerical limit (unlike the FRCP’s 25). Answers are due within the period stated in the interrogatories, not less than 30 days, each answer or objection must restate the interrogatory immediately before it, and general objections are not allowed and have no effect. A party may object to a failure to leave answer space by returning the interrogatories no later than the seventh day after receipt, or the objection is waived.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents, electronically stored information, and things; entry upon land","s":"A party may request production of documents, ESI, and tangible things or entry upon land, and the request may be served on a defendant with or after the summons and complaint. The response is due within the period stated in the request, not less than 30 days, general objections are barred, and each objection must say whether materials are being withheld. A request to a non-party must be served by subpoena, and a copy must first be served on all parties at least 10 days in advance (1 day if a hearing is set within that 10-day period or there is a bona fide emergency); a party may object within 10 days and must move to quash within 20 days after the objection, the non-party may respond within 30 days and is entitled to security against damages, and a party receiving non-party documents must serve copies (or give notice of availability) within 15 days.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examination of persons","s":"When a party’s mental or physical condition (including blood group), or that of a person in its custody or legal control, is in controversy, the court may on motion for good cause order an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner. On request, the party causing the examination must provide the examiner’s detailed report, and is then entitled to like reports from the examined party. Requesting the report or deposing the examiner waives privilege as to other examiners of the same condition.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Requests for admission may be served, without leave, no earlier than 10 days after the issues are first closed on the merits, or 30 days after the action begins if no responsive pleading is required, and must be in a separate document from any other pleading or discovery. A matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within the period stated in the request, not less than 30 days, and no motion to compel is needed for that result. General objections are barred, and a party who fails to admit something later proven must pay the costs of proof unless one of 4 listed exceptions applies.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make or cooperate in discovery: sanctions","s":"A party may move to compel discovery, and the loser of the motion must ordinarily pay the winner’s reasonable expenses, including attorney’s fees, unless the position was substantially justified. Failure to obey a discovery order may lead to facts being taken as established, exclusion of evidence, striking of pleadings, dismissal, default, or contempt, plus expenses. For lost ESI that should have been preserved and cannot be restored, the court may impose sanctions, including an adverse presumption or instruction, dismissal, or default, if prejudice results or the party intended to deprive another party of its use.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"Issues in causes that were exclusively equitable before June 18, 1852, are tried by the court, while other issues remain triable by jury as before. A party must file and serve a written jury demand no later than 10 days after the first responsive pleading to the complaint, counterclaim, cross-claim, or other claim (or within 10 days after that pleading would have been due). A late demand may be granted only on the written agreement of all parties filed with the court.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Issues on which a jury has been demanded are tried by jury unless the parties stipulate to a bench trial before evidence is admitted, or later if the court allows. On issues without a proper jury demand or jury right, the court may use an advisory jury, or with both sides’ consent may give the verdict binding effect. Findings of fact are not required to the extent the judge’s decision follows the verdict of a properly selected advisory jury.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"40","t":"Assignment of cases for trial","s":"Each trial court must adopt a rule for placing cases on the trial calendar, with precedence for actions entitled to it by statute, including hearings on temporary restraining orders, injunctions, and receiverships. The rule has no subdivision (B).","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by notice before the adverse party serves an answer or a motion for summary judgment, or by a stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared, and a second notice of dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. In a bench trial, after the party with the burden rests, the opposing party may move for involuntary dismissal on the weight of the evidence. When no action has been taken in a civil case for 60 days or the rules are not followed, the court must on motion or its own motion set a hearing and dismiss at the plaintiff’s costs unless sufficient cause is shown.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"The court may consolidate actions with common questions of law or fact, order separate trials, and submit a case to the jury in stages, such as bifurcating compensatory and punitive damages. Unlike the FRCP, actions with common questions pending in different Indiana courts may be consolidated for discovery and pretrial proceedings by motion in the court with the earliest-filed action, and are then returned unless the court finds unusual or complicated issues or a substantial question of great public importance. This cross-court consolidation does not apply to courts of limited jurisdiction, and the right to a change of venue (except for cause under IC 34-35-1-1) is suspended during consolidation.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"43","t":"Evidence","s":"Testimony is taken in open court unless state law or Supreme Court rules provide otherwise, and motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits, testimony, or depositions. The court may appoint and set compensation for an interpreter, in compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act. Unless the court directs otherwise, the party with the burden states its case first, then the adverse party, and evidence follows in the same order with rebuttal.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of official record","s":"Proof of official records is governed by the Indiana Rules of Evidence.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"The clerk issues signed and sealed blank subpoenas, and an Indiana attorney who has appeared for a party may also issue and sign subpoenas, which may be served by the sheriff, a party, or any person. A resident may be required to attend a deposition only in the county of residence, employment, or business, and a non-resident only in the county of service or within 40 miles of the place of service, unless the court orders another convenient place. Fees for 1 day’s attendance and mileage must be tendered to a witness required to attend outside the home county, except a party or a party organization’s officer, employee, or agent.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"46","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; it is enough to make known to the court the action desired or the objection and its grounds when the ruling is made or sought. A party who had no opportunity to object is not prejudiced by the lack of objection.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors and peremptory challenges","s":"Civil juries have 6 members, and the court may seat up to 3 alternate jurors. Each side has 3 peremptory challenges, plus 1 more if 1 or 2 alternates are seated or 2 more if 3 alternates are seated, and the extra challenges may be used only against alternates. Unlike the FRCP, the court must allow the parties or their attorneys to question prospective jurors, though it may set an advance time limit and must liberally extend it for good cause.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"48","t":"Juries of less than six; majority verdict","s":"The parties may stipulate, before the jury is selected, to a jury of fewer than 6 members, or, before the verdict is announced, that a verdict of a stated majority of jurors will be the verdict of the jury.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdicts and interrogatories","s":"Special verdicts and interrogatories to the jury are abolished, unlike FRCP 49.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment on the evidence (directed verdict)","s":"Indiana’s ‘judgment on the evidence’ replaces both the directed verdict and the JNOV: when issues are not supported by sufficient evidence or a verdict is clearly erroneous, the court must withdraw the issues from the jury or enter judgment notwithstanding the verdict. A party may move after the opponent rests, after the evidence on an issue or all evidence closes, or in a motion to correct error, and the court may act on its own before final judgment or before ruling on a motion to correct error; in civil cases (unlike criminal appeals) the issue may not be raised for the first time on appeal. The court may grant a new trial instead, and the motion for judgment notwithstanding the verdict, motion in arrest of judgment, demurrer to the evidence, and venire de novo are abolished.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to jury; objections; requests","s":"After the jury is sworn, the court gives preliminary instructions under Jury Rule 20, and the parties may object outside the jury’s presence; final instructions follow Jury Rule 26. Each party may tender no more than 10 requested instructions, including pattern instructions, each limited to 1 legal principle, unless the court allows more for good cause. A party must object to an instruction before the jury retires, stating the matter and grounds, and may request a pattern or model instruction simply by its number.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court","s":"In bench trials or trials with an advisory jury, the court must make special findings of fact and conclusions on its own motion or on a party’s written request filed before evidence is admitted, and must make them without request in preliminary injunction rulings, agency reviews, and where a rule or statute requires. Findings and judgment are set aside on appeal only if clearly erroneous. The court may amend findings or the judgment on its own motion before a motion to correct error is due, or with a motion to correct error, and failing to seek amendment does not waive the issue for a motion to correct error or appeal.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"53","t":"Court-appointed neutrals","s":"The court may appoint a court-appointed neutral (such as an attorney, senior judge, referee, auditor, examiner, commissioner, or other skilled person) if all parties consent, or without consent after notice and a hearing if it will materially help resolve the case and the costs are proportionate and not unduly burdensome; an attorney neutral must be licensed and in good standing in Indiana. Unless the order says otherwise, the neutral must set the first meeting within 20 days after the order of reference. In nonjury actions, any party may serve written objections to the neutral’s report within 10 days after notice of its filing.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"53.1","t":"Failure to rule on motion","s":"Indiana’s ‘lazy judge’ rule: if a court fails for 30 days to set a motion for hearing, or fails to rule within 30 days after hearing (or 30 days after filing if no hearing is required), a party may file a praecipe with notice to the Chief Administrative Officer of the Indiana Office of Judicial Administration to withdraw submission from the judge and have the Supreme Court appoint a special judge. ADR periods and post-hearing submission periods are excluded, the judge may extend the deadline once by 30 days by a CCS entry before it expires (further only with written consent of the parties), and the rule does not apply to repetitive motions, motions to reconsider, motions to correct error, post-conviction petitions, or ministerial post-judgment acts. A judge removed must file a report with the Supreme Court within 10 days, and a party who files anything further after the praecipe waives removal.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"53.2","t":"Time for holding issue under advisement; delay of entering a judgment","s":"If a cause tried to the court (including a post-conviction petition) is taken under advisement and the judge fails to decide any issue of law or fact within 90 days, submission may be withdrawn and the case sent to the Supreme Court for appointment of a special judge. ADR and post-hearing submission periods are excluded, the parties may agree on the record that the limit does not apply, and extensions, withdrawal procedure, and the judge’s report follow T.R. 53.1. Filing further papers after the praecipe waives removal, and the praecipe tolls all time limits until resolved.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"53.3","t":"Motion to correct error: time limitation for ruling","s":"A motion to correct error (Indiana’s post-trial motion under T.R. 59) is deemed denied if the court fails for 45 days to set it for hearing, or fails to rule within 30 days after the hearing or 45 days after filing if no hearing is required. A notice of appeal must then be filed under Appellate Rule 9(A) within 30 days after the deemed denial. The judge may extend the ruling time once by up to 30 days by a written CCS entry served on all parties before the initial period expires, and the parties may agree on the record that the limit does not apply.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"53.4","t":"Repetitive motions and motions to reconsider; automatic denial","s":"No hearing is required on a repetitive motion or a motion to reconsider, and such a motion does not delay the trial or extend any other deadline. Unless ruled on within 5 days, it is deemed denied without notice. The rule does not apply to an original motion for judgment on the evidence after the jury is discharged, a T.R. 52(B) motion to amend findings, an original motion to correct error, a T.R. 60 motion for relief from judgment, or original motions expressly designated as extending time.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"53.5","t":"[Vacated]","s":"Vacated effective January 1, 2025.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgment; costs","s":"A “judgment” includes a decree and any appealable order; in a case with multiple claims or parties, a judgment on fewer than all is final only if the court in writing expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and expressly directs entry of judgment. A default judgment may not differ in kind from or exceed the amount demanded, but other final judgments grant all relief the prevailing party is entitled to. Costs go to the prevailing party as of course (against governmental organizations only as permitted by law), the clerk may tax costs on 1 day’s notice with court review on motion served within 5 days, and judgments against 2 or more persons or on 2 or more claims are treated as joint and several for enforcement and challenge.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"A party who fails to plead or otherwise comply with the rules may be defaulted by the court, and the party seeking a default judgment must apply to the court; no default judgment may be entered against a known infant or incompetent unless represented by a general guardian or similar representative who has appeared, or against a person protected by the Servicemembers Civil Relief Act unless the Act is satisfied. A defaulted party who has appeared must be served with written notice of the application at least 3 days before the hearing, and the court may hold a hearing to determine damages, with a jury where required. A default judgment may be set aside under Rule 60(B), is subject to Rule 54(C), and may be entered against a governmental organization.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant may move for summary judgment any time after 20 days from commencement of the action (or after the adverse party moves), and a defending party may move at any time; the adverse party has 30 days after service of the motion to respond, and on a motion made no later than 10 days after the response was filed or due the court must hold a hearing set at least 10 days after the response deadline. Each side must designate to the court the specific evidence it relies on, and the opponent must designate each material fact issue that precludes judgment; the court decides only from designated material, may not grant judgment merely because no opposing affidavits were filed, and a judgment will not be reversed on a fact issue that was not specifically designated. Partial summary judgment is interlocutory unless the court in writing finds no just reason for delay and directs entry of judgment, denial may be challenged by a motion to correct error after final judgment, and the court may alter any time limit for cause on a motion made within that limit.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Declaratory judgment actions follow these rules, with jury trial available as provided in Rules 38 and 39. Another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, no property right need be involved, affirmative relief may be granted once the right is established, and the court may order a speedy hearing and advance the case on its calendar.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry and content of judgment","s":"The court must promptly prepare and sign the judgment on a separate document (or on the same document as its findings or opinion), and the clerk enters it in the Record of Judgments and Orders and notes it in the Chronological Case Summary and Judgment Docket; entry is not delayed for taxing costs, and a judge who fails to act may be compelled by mandate. Except in small claims, a judgment must state the nature of the submission, the appearances, the relief granted in imperative form, and the date and the judge’s signature, and may state jurisdiction and the issues decided. On full payment the judgment creditor must file a satisfaction/release; if the clerk issues a notice that the judgment appears paid, the creditor has 30 days to file a verified objection or the judgment is deemed satisfied.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"59","t":"Motion to correct error","s":"Indiana uses a single motion to correct error in place of the federal motions for new trial and to alter or amend; it may be filed by any party or by the court, and must be filed within 30 days after entry of the final judgment is noted in the Chronological Case Summary. The motion is not a prerequisite to appeal except to raise newly discovered material evidence (including jury misconduct) that could be produced within 30 days of final judgment, or a claim that a jury verdict is excessive or inadequate; errors must be stated specifically with supporting facts, an opponent may file a statement in opposition within 15 days after service, and a motion based on evidence outside the record must be supported by affidavits, with opposing affidavits or a counter-motion due within 15 days and no reply allowed. The court may grant a new trial, enter judgment, alter or amend the judgment, or order additur or remittitur, must give general reasons for relief and special findings when granting a new trial on the evidence, and an order modifying or setting aside a judgment is itself appealable; no motion is allowed for certain juvenile placement and services orders, which are appealed under Appellate Rule 14.1.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected by the trial court at any time before the Notice of Completion of Clerk’s Record is filed, and afterward with leave of the court on appeal. Relief may be granted for (1) mistake, surprise, or excusable neglect, (2) grounds for a motion to correct error such as newly discovered evidence, (3) fraud or misconduct, (4) default against a party served only by publication without actual knowledge, (5) judgment against an unrepresented infant or incompetent (motion within 90 days after the disability is removed or a guardian appointed), (6) void judgment, (7) satisfied, released, or no longer equitable judgment, or (8) any other justifying reason; motions on grounds (1)-(4) must be filed within 1 year, the others within a reasonable time, and grounds (1)-(4) and (8) require a meritorious claim or defense. A ruling on the motion is a final appealable judgment, no change of venue is allowed except for cause shown by affidavit, and the time limits are not tolled for infants, incompetents, or governmental organizations.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"60.5","t":"Mandate of funds","s":"A trial court (other than the Supreme Court or Court of Appeals) seeking to compel a municipality, political subdivision, or officer to appropriate or pay funds reasonably necessary for court operations must first meet with the mandated party and then issue an “Order for Mandate of Funds” to show cause, followed by a bench trial before a special judge appointed by the Supreme Court who is not a current or former regular judge and does not reside or regularly practice in that county or a contiguous one; there is no change of venue, and an appointee who fails to qualify within 7 days is replaced. Unless the respondent waives review in writing within 30 days, the decree is automatically reviewed by the Supreme Court, no motion to correct error or notice of appeal is filed, and no mandate order is effective until reviewed or review is waived. Mandate will not lie for extravagant or personal expenses, attorney fees may not exceed the reasonable customary hourly rate in the county, and the dispute may be sent to mediation at any time.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"No error in admitting or excluding evidence, or in any ruling or act of the court or parties, is grounds for correcting error, setting aside a verdict, disturbing a judgment, or reversal unless refusing to act would be inconsistent with substantial justice. The court must disregard any error that does not affect the parties’ substantial rights.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Execution may issue once the judgment is noted in the Chronological Case Summary, but the court may stay enforcement pending a motion to correct error, a Rule 50, 52, or 60 motion, or an appeal, and may suspend, modify, or grant injunctions or receivership orders during an appeal on terms it sets. No bond is needed to perfect an appeal, but enforcement is stayed on appeal only on approval of an appeal bond, an irrevocable letter of credit, or other approved security, generally in an amount covering the unpaid judgment, appeal costs, interest, and damages for delay (or, for property judgments, the value of use and detention, costs, interest, and damages), unless the court for good cause sets a different amount. Governmental organizations and court-appointed representatives get a stay without bond, money or a check may be furnished in lieu of a bond, and a court entering a Rule 54(B) partial judgment may stay its enforcement until later judgments are entered.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"63","t":"Disability and unavailability of a judge","s":"The judge who presided at a trial or evidentiary hearing should decide post-trial matters, but if that judge is unavailable through death, sickness, absence, or unwillingness, another judge of the circuit or assigned to the case may act and may grant a new trial in whole or part if unable to perform the duties. On a verified petition from the judge, or from any person alleging the judge is disabled or neglecting the duties of office, the Supreme Court may appoint a full-time judge pro tempore (a show-cause order to the judge must be served at least 10 days before the response date), and that judge pro tempore must be an attorney in good standing and is paid at the regular judge’s salary rate. A judge unable to attend court may appoint a judge pro tempore in writing, and a locally appointed judge pro tempore is paid $25 for each day or part of a day served.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"63.1","t":"Lis pendens notice of proceedings avoiding judgments; assignments and discharges","s":"Post-judgment proceedings to avoid a judgment (under Rules 50, 52, 59, 60(B), or the appellate rules) and the tolling or extension of a limitations period are ineffective against a good-faith purchaser of land, or a purchaser or lien creditor of personal property, unless lis pendens notice is filed in the county lis pendens record (for land) or by a financing statement (for personal property); the notice must be signed, identify the judgment by court and docket number, name the parties, and describe the property. Satisfactions, releases, and assignments of docketed judgments and lis pendens entries may be recorded in the same manner as the original if acknowledged like a deed, certified by the clerk, or entered on the margin of the record and attested by the clerk. Creditor proceedings and judicial liens against personal property give constructive notice only upon possession or the filing of a financing statement meeting stated requirements, subject to UCC Article 9 duration rules, and a properly filed notice stays perfected for the life of the resulting judgment.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizure of person or property","s":"All remedies for seizing persons or property to secure a judgment (arrest, attachment, garnishment, lis pendens, replevin, ejectment, sequestration, and the like) are available as provided by law; a writ of attachment, bench warrant, or body attachment may issue only after a rule to show cause is personally served on the judgment debtor (including certified mail signed by the debtor), a return of service is filed, and the debtor fails to appear. A person taken into custody in a civil action must be brought before a judicial officer within 48 hours, excluding weekends and holidays, and a writ of attachment for a person expires 180 days after issuance. Pre-judgment attachment and garnishment are also allowed against foreign corporations, nonresidents, or defendants whose whereabouts are unknown, on any money claim, though wages are not subject to pre-judgment garnishment except as provided by law; a defendant who denies the plaintiff’s title puts the defendant’s own title in issue, after which the plaintiff may not voluntarily dismiss without prejudice unless the defendant joins.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"No preliminary injunction may issue without notice and an opportunity for a hearing, the court may consolidate the hearing with trial on the merits, and if no order is entered within 10 days after the hearing the matter becomes subject to Rule 53.1; if the assigned judge is unavailable, a party may apply by affidavit to another judge in the circuit or an adjoining circuit. A temporary restraining order without notice requires specific facts showing immediate irreparable injury and an attorney’s certification of notice efforts, expires within the time fixed (not more than 10 days) unless extended for a like period, and may be challenged on 2 days’ notice; security is required except from a governmental organization, and every order must include Rule 52 findings, be specific, and bind only parties and those in active concert who receive actual notice. In dissolution, separation, or child support cases the court may issue a joint order without hearing or security on a verified petition barring disposal of marital property, removing a child from Indiana, or changing insurance, but orders against abuse or harassment or excluding a party from a dwelling must be issued separately against one person (no mutual orders), and these orders take effect on service and last until a decree or final order.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Security: proceedings against sureties","s":"A surety on any bond or undertaking given in a court proceeding submits to the court’s jurisdiction and irrevocably appoints the clerk as agent for service. The surety’s liability may be enforced by motion without a separate action, with the clerk mailing copies to sureties whose addresses are known; this applies to appeal bonds, enforced in the court to which the case returns.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers, assignees, and liquidators; claims","s":"An action in which a receiver, assignee for the benefit of creditors, or other liquidator is appointed may not be dismissed without court order, and the court may (or on petition must) set a deadline for the debtor or its officers to file an itemized affidavit of all assets, liabilities, and known creditors, which the liquidator prepares if they do not. The officer must give notice of appointment by publication and mail it to listed creditors, setting a claims period of not less than 6 months from appointment, and claims procedure follows that for decedents’ estates. Liquidated claims shown as unpaid on the debtor’s regular books must be paid by priority even if not filed, subject to recovery of overpayments.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in court; payment of judgment","s":"Before judgment in an action involving money or a deliverable thing, a party may, on notice and with leave of court, deposit all or part of it with the court for later payment as the court directs. A money judgment may be paid to the judgment creditor or the creditor’s attorney, the sheriff holding execution, or the clerk, and the person receiving payment must furnish a signed, acknowledged statement of full or partial satisfaction identifying the judgment by cause number, which may be filed under Rule 63.1(B) as constructive notice.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"A party defending against a claim may serve an offer of judgment, with costs then accrued, at any time more than 10 days before trial begins (the federal rule uses 14 days), and the offeree has 10 days after service to accept in writing, after which either party may file the offer and acceptance and the clerk enters judgment. An unaccepted offer is deemed withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs, and if the offeree’s final judgment is not more favorable than the offer the offeree must pay costs incurred after the offer; a later offer is still permitted. Once liability is determined but the amount is not, the liable party may make an offer with the same effect if served within a reasonable time not less than 10 days before the damages hearing.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution, proceedings supplemental, foreclosure sales","s":"Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution, but real estate may not be sold until 6 months after the judgment or execution becomes a lien, and execution sales of real estate follow mortgage-foreclosure procedures without a right of redemption after sale; judgment and execution liens on after-acquired property share pro rata, and other judicial sales generally follow the procedure for sales in decedents’ estates. Proceedings supplemental are started by verified motion or affidavit in the court that entered the judgment, the court orders the debtor and any garnishee to appear or answer interrogatories ex parte, and the appearance or answer date must be at least 20 days after service; the debtor is served under Rule 5 and garnishees under Rule 4, and the parties may use discovery. In any judicial sale of land the court may order a title opinion or title insurance policy covering the purchase price, paid as a cost of sale.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title; recordation","s":"If a party fails to perform an act ordered by judgment, such as conveying land or delivering documents, the court may have another person perform it at the disobedient party’s cost with the same effect, may issue attachment, assistance, or sequestration, may hold the party in contempt and award damages, or may simply enter a judgment divesting and vesting title. A judgment for possession entitles the party to a writ directing the sheriff to deliver possession, and equitable orders to pay money are enforced as money judgments. Such judgments or conveyances may be recorded in the lis pendens or deed records as constructive notice, and the rule supplies a form of deed for a court appointee.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"71","t":"Process for and against nonparties","s":"A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it by the same process as a party. A nonparty against whom an order may lawfully be enforced is subject to the same enforcement process as a party.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"72","t":"Trial courts and clerks","s":"Trial courts are always open for filing papers and issuing process, terms of court are not recognized, trials on the merits are held in open court, and other proceedings may be held in chambers anywhere, though no hearing other than ex parte may be held outside Indiana without all affected parties’ consent. The clerk’s office is open on business days, and the clerk must immediately serve a copy of every ruling, order, or judgment noted in the Chronological Case Summary on each party not in default (in consolidated cases with 10 or more parties, the judge may order notice through liaison parties); attorneys and unrepresented parties must list a mailing address and an email address, and service at either is sufficient. Lack of notice does not extend the time to challenge a ruling, except that if the clerk’s service is not noted in the CCS the court may, for good cause, extend the time for a party lacking actual knowledge, starting from actual knowledge and not exceeding the original period.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"73","t":"Hearing of motions","s":"Each judge should set regular times and places for hearing motions, and may order the advancement and hearing of actions at any time on reasonable notice. The court may decide motions without oral hearing on brief written statements, or hold hearings by telephone conference or similar means.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"74","t":"Recording proceedings; transcripts; audio recordings","s":"Circuit, superior, probate, city, town, and Marion County Small Claims courts must audio-record all hearings and trials in all case types, including all testimony, rulings, and objections; stenography is allowed if the audio requirement is also met. A transcript for appeal is requested through the Notice of Appeal under the Appellate Rules, and transcripts or audio copies for other purposes are requested by written request. Court reporters’ powers, duties, and salaries are governed by Indiana Code 33-41.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"75","t":"Venue requirements","s":"Any case may be filed and decided in any court in any county, but on a timely Rule 12(B)(3) motion or pleading the court must transfer the case to a county or court of “preferred venue” selected by the first party properly moving; preferred venue includes, among 10 listed categories, the county where most individual defendants reside, where the land or chattels are located, where a vehicle accident occurred, where a defendant organization’s principal or relevant office is, by written stipulation of all parties, or (as a fallback) where a plaintiff resides or has its office. A claim filed in the wrong Indiana court must be transferred, not dismissed, and the filer must pay transfer costs within 20 days or the case is dismissed without prejudice with attorney fees to the objecting party; on transfer the filer pays refiling costs and the opponents’ mileage, plus attorney fees if the case was filed in the wrong county in bad faith or without cause. The rule supersedes other venue statutes, venue is not jurisdictional, and a transfer order is an interlocutory order appealable under Appellate Rule 14(A)(8) without an automatic stay.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"76","t":"Change of venue or judge in civil actions","s":"Each party in a civil case is entitled to one change of judge, granted automatically on an unverified motion stating no grounds, with the new judge chosen under Rule 79; a change of venue from the county requires a verified motion showing the county is a party or that local prejudice makes a fair trial unlikely, and denial on fair-trial grounds is reviewed only for abuse of discretion. After venue change is granted, the parties have 10 days to agree on a county; otherwise the court must within 5 days list 3 adjoining or same-district counties, and the parties strike alternately within 5 days each, the movant first, with the clerk striking for a party who fails to. Motions must be filed within 10 days after the issues are first closed on the merits, except 30 days from filing where no responsive pleading closes the issues (and in probate, receivership, and remonstrance matters), 10 days after a new trial order or remand, 10 days after a transferred case is given a new case number, or later on a personally verified motion explaining when and how newly discovered grounds were found and why they could not have been discovered sooner (opponents may respond within 10 days). A party is limited to one change of county and one change of judge in all civil cases, including petitions to modify a final decree; the rule applies to CHINS and termination cases, while infractions follow the Criminal Rules.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"77","t":"Court records","s":"The circuit court clerk keeps the records for all circuit, superior, and probate courts in the county, including statutory records (lis pendens, judgment docket, execution docket, and others), the Chronological Case Summary (CCS), case files, the Record of Judgments and Orders (RJO), and indexes, in paper, microfilm, or electronic form. The judge must cause prompt CCS entries of all judicial events, dated the day the notation is made, and orders entered in the judgment docket must include “judgment” in the title and the specific dollar amount; a separate RJO is not needed if the court has an approved scanning system. The rule also governs case-file status, statistics, replacement of lost papers, approval of record systems by the Office of Judicial Administration, and electronic posting of court records with judicial consent.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"78","t":"Jurisdiction pending change from county","s":"Once a change of venue to another county is ordered and any required costs are paid, either party may file a certified copy of the order in the receiving court, which then has full jurisdiction even before the transcript and papers arrive. The original court keeps jurisdiction to decide emergency matters between the filing of the venue motion and the order granting it.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"79","t":"Special judge selection: circuit, superior, and probate courts","s":"This rule is the exclusive method for selecting a special judge in circuit, superior, and probate court civil and juvenile cases after a change of judge under Rule 76 or a mandatory recusal (when the judge, the judge’s spouse, a relative within the third degree, or a household member is a party, a lawyer in the case, has an interest that could be substantially affected, or is otherwise disqualified under the Code of Judicial Conduct), and parties must promptly notify the court of a change-of-judge motion. Within 7 days after the change or disqualification order is noted in the CCS, the parties may agree in writing on an eligible special judge, who has 7 days from the CCS notation of the appointment to accept, and filing the acceptance vests jurisdiction without an oath; if there is no agreement or acceptance, the judge is appointed under a Supreme Court–approved local rule from the administrative district or a contiguous county, must accept unless disqualified or excused, and difficult cases are certified to the Supreme Court, which may appoint any eligible judge or member of the bar. Eligible judges include regular circuit, superior, and probate judges, senior judges, and full-time judicial officers who have not previously served in the case and are not disqualified, and the special judge keeps the case through judgment and post-judgment proceedings; if a special judge stops serving, the regular judge resumes if eligible, and if the special judge is unavailable on a hearing date a pro tem, magistrate, senior judge, or the regular judge may sit or the hearing is reset. Hearings with live testimony are held in the court where the case is pending unless agreed or exceptional circumstances exist, the original judge keeps emergency jurisdiction until the special judge accepts, full-time judicial employees receive no special judge fee, senior judges are paid under Administrative Rule 5, and others receive $25 per day plus mileage and expenses if from out of county.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"79.1","t":"Special judge selection: city, town, and Marion County small claims courts","s":"This rule is the exclusive method for selecting special judges in city, town, and Marion County small claims courts, and unlike Rule 76(B) a motion for change of judge in these courts must be verified and signed by the party, stating facts supporting the statutory basis. The parties may agree on an eligible special judge, or consent to selection by the presiding judge, and the selected judge has 20 days to accept jurisdiction, appear, and qualify, without special judge fees; if no agreement is reached, a city or town court case is transferred without added fees to the county’s superior or circuit court. In Marion County small claims court, the presiding judge must within 2 days name a panel of 3 other small claims judges, each party strikes one (the movant first) within a period of 7 to 14 days set by the court, the presiding judge resumes jurisdiction if the movant fails to strike and the clerk strikes for a non-moving party who fails, and the case is transferred without costs to the remaining judge; only small claims judges may serve there, and the special judge or receiving court keeps the case for all future proceedings.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"80","t":"Procedure for amending rules","s":"Proposed amendments to the Indiana Rules of Court are submitted to the Supreme Court’s Chief Administrative Officer in a Word-compatible format with added and deleted language marked, along with the form on the Court’s website, and are referred to the appropriate committee. The Rules Committee publishes proposals on or before January 2, April 1, July 1, and October 1 each year (and at other times as needed), accepts written comments, which are confidential unless the Court orders otherwise, for 30 days (extendable), and then submits final drafts to the Supreme Court. Adopted amendments take effect on January 1 of the following year unless the Court orders otherwise.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"81","t":"Local court rules","s":"Courts and administrative districts may adopt local rules that neither conflict with nor duplicate the statewide rules, but may not regulate practice by standing orders. Proposed local rules must be noticed to the bar and public (including local bar association officers), sent to the county clerk and the Office of Judicial Administration for posting, and open for comment for at least 30 days on a uniform annual schedule; a rule adopted off-schedule for good cause does not take effect until posted for 30 days. Adopted rules must be compiled and posted free on the Indiana Judicial Website, may be waived or modified in an individual case with the waiver noted in the CCS, and must be reviewed at least once every 2 years.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"81.1","t":"Procedures for cases involving family or household members","s":"Courts may use “Family Procedures” to coordinate and hear together cases involving family or household members (current or former spouses or domestic partners, those with a child in common, blood or adoptive relatives, and guardians, wards, custodians, or foster parents), keeping separate case numbers or using a common number before one judge, though guardianship cases may not be consolidated into a common number. The court must give all parties notice of its intent and, within 30 days after a case is selected, a list of the selected cases; a party has 15 days after notice to object for cause, and after notice a change of judge may be granted only for cause. The court may hold concurrent hearings, takes judicial notice under Evidence Rule 201, preserves the confidentiality of excluded records, and must consolidate juvenile paternity cases involving children of the same two parents into the first-filed case.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"82","t":"Forms","s":"Forms adopted by the Supreme Court or the Coalition for Court Access are sufficient under the rules. They must be accepted for filing, subject to Rule 88.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"83","t":"Definitions","s":"Defines “court on appeal” (the Indiana Supreme Court or Court of Appeals), “executive” and “executive officer” of an organization (including, for service purposes, a personal secretary or employee entrusted with legal papers), “governmental organization,” “governmental representative,” “organization,” and “representative.” “Signature” or “signed” includes an electronic reproduction of a handwritten signature.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"84","t":"[Vacated]","s":"Vacated, effective July 15, 2021.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"85","t":"[Vacated]","s":"Vacated, effective February 1, 1996.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"86","t":"General electronic filing and electronic service","s":"Defines the terms used in Rules 86-88, including the Indiana E-Filing System (IEFS), E-Filing (which excludes fax and email), E-Filing Service Providers, the Notice of Electronic Filing (NEF), and Users, who must sign a User Agreement with an approved provider. When filing the initial complaint, the User also files completed summonses, which the clerk dates, signs, seals, and returns for the User to serve (the clerk serves in prosecutor-initiated cases and when fees are waived), and returns of service may go to the clerk or the User. After the complaint, all papers required to be served under Rule 5(A) must be served on Users by E-Service through the IEFS, which is complete on transmission to the email on the appearance or Public Service List; non-Users and exempt parties are served under Rules 4 and 5, protection order and child protection order cases must be e-filed through the Protection Order Registry provider and served by the clerk, sheriff, or a court-appointed person, and the electronic version of a filed document is the official record.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"87","t":"Electronic filing","s":"An action must be commenced through the IEFS (unless exempt) by paying the filing fee (unless waived) and filing the complaint with the required summonses, and all documents must be e-filed unless the rules provide otherwise; an attorney may be exempted only by a petition filed in each pending case and granted for good cause, while unrepresented litigants are not required (though encouraged) to e-file until the Supreme Court orders otherwise. A document is filed at the date and time on its NEF, must be filed before midnight to count for that day (measured in the court’s time zone), e-filing is available 24 hours a day except for maintenance, signatures may be a graphic image or “/s/” followed by the name, and conventionally filed documents are scanned by the clerk and must be conventionally served with a certificate of service. If an IEFS failure prevents filing, a User may file conventionally and deadlines (other than those that cannot be extended by law) are extended 1 day for each day of failure, other failures not caused by the User may be addressed under Rule 6(B), and a filer of a will for probate e-files a copy with an affidavit and keeps the original until the estate closes or the contest period expires, whichever is later.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"},{"n":"88","t":"Court and clerk electronic filing review","s":"The clerk may reject an e-filed document only if the filing fee was not paid, the wrong case management system was selected, or the User requested rejection; a document that cannot be processed electronically is returned and may be cured within 3 business days, and other non-conforming documents are processed and then returned for correction. If the court finds an e-filed document non-conforming, it must issue an order allowing a cure within 3 business days, and a cured document is deemed filed as of the original filing date.","u":"https://rules.incourts.gov/Content/trial/default.htm"}]});
